window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-12-1", "source": "2021年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay related to the short passage given below. In your essay, you are to comment on the phenomenon described in the passage and suggest measures to address the issue. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.\n\nYoung people spend a lot of time on the Internet. However, they are sometimes unable to recognize false information on the Internet, judge the reliability of online information sources, or tell real news stories from fake ones.", "sample": "It has become a common phenomenon that young people spend a great deal of time online, yet many of them\nstruggle to tell trustworthy information from falsehood. As the passage notes, they are often unable to\nrecognize false information, judge the reliability of online sources, or distinguish real news from fake stories.\nThis deficiency in digital judgment, though easily overlooked, deserves our serious attention.\nThe consequences are far from trivial. Misled by fabricated news, young people may form distorted views of\nthe world, make ill-informed decisions, and even spread rumours unknowingly. Since their critical-thinking\nskills are still developing, they are especially vulnerable to eye-catching yet groundless claims that circulate\nrapidly across social media.\nTo address this issue, concerted efforts are needed. First and foremost, schools should offer media-literacy\ncourses that teach students how to verify facts and trace information back to its source. Meanwhile, online\nplatforms ought to strengthen fact-checking and clearly label questionable content. Finally, young people\nthemselves should cultivate the habit of cross-checking a story against several reliable sources before believing\nor sharing it. Only in this way can they navigate the digital world wisely."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国共产党第一次全国代表大会会址位于上海兴业路76号，是一栋典型的上海式住宅，建于1920年秋。1921年7月23日，中国共产党第一次全国代表大会在此召开，大会通过了中国共产党的第一个纲领和第一个决议，选举产生了中央领导机构，宣告了中国共产党的诞生。1952年9月，中共一大会址修复，建立纪念馆并对外开放。纪念馆除了介绍参加一大的代表之外，还介绍党的历史发展进程，现已成为了解党史，缅怀革命先烈的爱国主义教育基地。", "sample": "The site of the First National Congress of the Communist Party of China, located at No. 76 Xingye Road in Shanghai, is a typical Shanghai-style residence built in the autumn of 1920. On July 23, 1921, the First National Congress of the Communist Party of China was held here, where the Party adopted its first program and its first resolution, elected its central leading body, and proclaimed the founding of the Communist Party of China. In September 1952, the site was restored, turned into a memorial hall, and opened to the public. In addition to introducing the delegates who attended the Congress, the memorial hall also presents the course of the Party's historical development. It has now become a base for patriotism education, where people can learn about the Party's history and commemorate revolutionary martyrs."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2021-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "If you think life is wonderful and expect it to stay that way, then you may have a good chance of living to a ripe old age, at least that is what the findings of a new study suggest. That study found that participants who reported the highest levels of optimism were far more likely to live to age 85 or  __26__  . This was compared to those participants who reported the lowest levels of optimism. It is  __27__  that the findings held even after the researchers considered factors that could  __28__  the link, including whether participants had health conditions such as heart disease or cancer, or whether they experienced depression. The results add to a growing body of evidence that certain psychological factors may predict a longer life  __29__  . For example, previous studies have found that more optimistic people have a lower risk of developing chronic diseases, and a lower risk of  __30__  death. However, the new study appears to be the first to  __31__  look at the relationship between optimism and longevity. The researchers  __32__  that the link found in the new study was not as strong when they factored in the effects of certain health behaviors, including exercise levels, sleep habits and diet. This suggests that these behaviors may, at least in part, explain the link. In other words, optimism may  __33__  good habits that bolster health. It is also important to note that the study found only a  __34__  , as researchers did not prove for certain that optimism leads to a longer life. However, if the findings are true, they suggest that optimism could serve as a psychological  __35__  that promotes health and a longer life.", "matchIntro": "No one in fashion is surprised that Burberry burnt £28 million of stock", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Last week, Burberry's annual report revealed that £28.6 million worth of stock was burnt last year. The news has left investors and consumers outraged but comes as little surprise to those in the fashion industry."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The practice of destroying unsold stock, and even rolls of unused fabric, is commonplace for luxury labels. Becoming too widely available at a cheaper price through discount stores discourages full-price sales. Sending products for recycling leaves them vulnerable to being stolen and sold on the black market. Jasmine Bina, CEO of brand strategy agency Concept Bureau explains, \"Typically, luxury brands rally around exclusivity to protect their business interests, namely intellectual property and preservation of brand equity(资产).\" She stated she had heard rumors of stock burning but not specific cases until this week."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Another reason for the commonplace practice is a financial incentive for brands exporting goods to America. United States Customs states that if imported merchandise is unused and destroyed under their supervision, 99% of the duties, taxes or fees paid on the merchandise may be recovered. It is incredibly difficult to calculate how much dead stock currently goes to waste. While there are incentives to do it, there's no legal obligation to report it."}, {"label": "D", "text": "A source, who chose to remain anonymous, shared her experience working in a Burberry store in New York in October 2016. \"My job was to toss items in boxes so they could be sent to be burned. It was killing me inside because all that leather and fur went to waste and animals had died for nothing. I couldn't stay there any longer, their business practices threw me off the roof.\" In May this year, Burberry announced it was taking fur out of its catwalk shows and reviewing its use elsewhere in the business. \"Even though we asked the management, they refused to give us detailed answers as to why they would do this with their collection,\" continued the source, who left her role within two weeks. She has since worked with another high-profile, luxury label."}, {"label": "E", "text": "In an online forum post, which asked if it's true that Louis Vuitton burned its bags, Ahmed Bouchfaa, who claimed to work for Louis Vuitton, responded that the brand holds sales of old stock for staff members twice a year. Items which have still not sold after several sales are destroyed. \"Louis Vuitton doesn't have public sales. They either sell a product at a given price or discontinue it. This is to make sure that everybody pays the same price for an item,\" he says. He goes on to disclose the strict guidelines around the employee sales: \"You may buy gifts for someone, but they track each item, and if your gift ends up online they know who to ask.\" One investor commenting on the Burberry figures was reportedly outraged that the unsold goods were not even offered to investors before they were destroyed."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Richemont, who owns several luxury brands, hit the headlines in May for taking back £437 million of watches for destruction in the last two years to avoid marked-down prices. It's not just luxury brands either. In October last year, a Danish TV show exposed H&M for burning 12 tonnes of unsold clothing since 2013. In a statement, the high street retailer defended itself by saying that the burnt clothing had failed safety tests: \"The products to which the media are referring have been tested in external laboratories. The test results show that one of the products is mold infested and the other product contains levels of lead that are too high. Those products have rightly been stopped in accordance with our safety routines.\" In March, a report revealed that H&M was struggling with $4.3 billion worth of unsold stock. The brand told The New York Times that the plan was to reduce prices to move the stock, arguably encouraging consumers to buy and throw away with little thought."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Over-production is perhaps the biggest concern for Burberry. While there has been much outrage at the elitist connotation of burning goods rather than making them affordable, executives at the British fashion house are no doubt struggling to defend how they miscalculated production. The waste has been put down to burning old cosmetic stock to make way for their new beauty range. However, while the value of destroyed stock is up from £26.9 million last year, it's an even more significant increase from 2016's figure of £18.8 million, highlighting that this is an ongoing issue."}, {"label": "H", "text": "In September 2016, Burberry switched to a \"see now, buy now\" catwalk show format. The move was a switch to leverage on the coverage of their fashion week show to make stock available immediately to consumers. This is opposed to the traditional format of presenting to the industry, taking orders for production and becoming available in six months' time. While Burberry announced \"record-breaking\" online reach and engagement, there has been little evidence to suggest that the strategy has had a significant effect on sales, particularly as the hype(炒作) slows across the season. In February they made adjustments to the format, dropping some catwalk items immediately and promising that others would launch in the coming months."}, {"label": "I", "text": "In a statement, Burberry denied that switching to \"see now, buy now\" has had an impact on waste. A Burberry spokesperson further said, \"On the occasions when disposal of products is necessary, we do so in a responsible manner. We are always seeking ways to reduce and revalue our waste. This is a core part of our strategy and we have forged partnerships and committed support to innovative organizations to help reach this goal.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "One such partnership is with Elvis & Kresse, an accessories brand working with reclaimed materials. Co-founder Kresse Wesling said, \"Late last year we launched an ambitious five-year partnership with the Burberry Foundation. The main aim of this is to scale our leather rescue project, starting with off-cuts from the production of Burberry leather goods. We are working tirelessly to expand our solutions and would love to welcome anyone to our workshop to come and see what we are doing.\" At the moment, the partnership only addresses waste at the production stage and not unsold goods."}, {"label": "K", "text": "While these are honorable schemes, it makes it harder for Burberry to defend these latest figures. Fifteen years ago, Burberry was at crisis point as their signature check pattern was widely imitated by cheap, imitation brands. It deterred luxury consumers who found their expensive clothing more closely associated with working-class youth culture than a prestigious heritage fashion house. In the year 2004, at the height of over-exposure of the Burberry check, the brand's turnover was £715.5 million. Under Christopher Bailey as creative director they turned the brand around and this past year revenue hit £2.73 billion."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Bina believes that brands need to readdress their exclusivity tactic. \"Exclusivity is starting to be challenged,\" she says, \"I think that goes hand in hand with how luxury itself is being challenged. Access to fashion, and the brands who police it, are becoming less and less relevant. Things like health, enlightenment, and social and environmental responsibility are the new luxuries. These all come from within, not without. That's the challenge that traditional luxury brands will have to contend with in the mid- to long-term future.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Social media is absolutely everywhere. Billions of people use social media on a daily basis to create, share, and exchange ideas, messages, and information. Both individuals and businesses post regularly to engage and interact with people from around the world. It is a powerful communication medium that simultaneously provides immediate, frequent, permanent, and wide-reaching information across the globe.\nPeople post their lives on social media for the world to see. Facebook, Twitter, LinkedIn, and countless other social channels provide a quick and simple way to glimpse into a job candidate's personal life—both the positive and negative sides of it. Social media screening is tempting to use as part of the hiring process, but should employers make use of it when researching a potential candidate's background?\nIncorporating the use of social media to screen job candidates is not an uncommon practice. A 2018 survey found that almost 70% of employers use social media to screen candidates before hiring them. But there are consequences and potential legal risks involved too. When done inappropriately, social media screening can be considered unethical or even illegal.\nSocial media screening is essentially scrutinising a job candidate's private life. It can reveal information about protected characteristics like age, race, nationality, disability, gender, religion, etc., and that could bias a hiring decision. Pictures or comments on a private page that are taken out of context could ruin a perfectly good candidate's chances of getting hired. This process could potentially give an unfair advantage to one candidate over another. It creates an unequal playing field and potentially provides hiring managers with information that can impact their hiring decision in a negative way.\nIt's hard to ignore social media as a screening tool. While there are things that you shouldn't see, there are some things that can be lawfully considered—making it a valuable source of relevant information too. Using social media screening appropriately can help ensure that you don't hire a toxic employee who will cost you money or stain your company's reputation. Consider the lawful side of this process and you may be able to hire the best employee ever. There is a delicate balance.\nScreening job candidates on social media must be done professionally and responsibly. Companies should stipulate that they will never ask for passwords, be consistent, document decisions, consider the source used and be aware that other laws may apply. In light of this, it is probably best to look later in the process and ask human resources for help in navigating it. Social media is here to stay. But before using social media to screen job candidates, consulting with management and legal teams beforehand is essential in order to comply with all laws.", "47": "Social media is absolutely everywhere. Billions of people use social media on a daily basis to create, share, and exchange ideas, messages, and information. Both individuals and businesses post regularly to engage and interact with people from around the world. It is a powerful communication medium that simultaneously provides immediate, frequent, permanent, and wide-reaching information across the globe.\nPeople post their lives on social media for the world to see. Facebook, Twitter, LinkedIn, and countless other social channels provide a quick and simple way to glimpse into a job candidate's personal life—both the positive and negative sides of it. Social media screening is tempting to use as part of the hiring process, but should employers make use of it when researching a potential candidate's background?\nIncorporating the use of social media to screen job candidates is not an uncommon practice. A 2018 survey found that almost 70% of employers use social media to screen candidates before hiring them. But there are consequences and potential legal risks involved too. When done inappropriately, social media screening can be considered unethical or even illegal.\nSocial media screening is essentially scrutinising a job candidate's private life. It can reveal information about protected characteristics like age, race, nationality, disability, gender, religion, etc., and that could bias a hiring decision. Pictures or comments on a private page that are taken out of context could ruin a perfectly good candidate's chances of getting hired. This process could potentially give an unfair advantage to one candidate over another. It creates an unequal playing field and potentially provides hiring managers with information that can impact their hiring decision in a negative way.\nIt's hard to ignore social media as a screening tool. While there are things that you shouldn't see, there are some things that can be lawfully considered—making it a valuable source of relevant information too. Using social media screening appropriately can help ensure that you don't hire a toxic employee who will cost you money or stain your company's reputation. Consider the lawful side of this process and you may be able to hire the best employee ever. There is a delicate balance.\nScreening job candidates on social media must be done professionally and responsibly. Companies should stipulate that they will never ask for passwords, be consistent, document decisions, consider the source used and be aware that other laws may apply. In light of this, it is probably best to look later in the process and ask human resources for help in navigating it. Social media is here to stay. But before using social media to screen job candidates, consulting with management and legal teams beforehand is essential in order to comply with all laws.", "48": "Social media is absolutely everywhere. Billions of people use social media on a daily basis to create, share, and exchange ideas, messages, and information. Both individuals and businesses post regularly to engage and interact with people from around the world. It is a powerful communication medium that simultaneously provides immediate, frequent, permanent, and wide-reaching information across the globe.\nPeople post their lives on social media for the world to see. Facebook, Twitter, LinkedIn, and countless other social channels provide a quick and simple way to glimpse into a job candidate's personal life—both the positive and negative sides of it. Social media screening is tempting to use as part of the hiring process, but should employers make use of it when researching a potential candidate's background?\nIncorporating the use of social media to screen job candidates is not an uncommon practice. A 2018 survey found that almost 70% of employers use social media to screen candidates before hiring them. But there are consequences and potential legal risks involved too. When done inappropriately, social media screening can be considered unethical or even illegal.\nSocial media screening is essentially scrutinising a job candidate's private life. It can reveal information about protected characteristics like age, race, nationality, disability, gender, religion, etc., and that could bias a hiring decision. Pictures or comments on a private page that are taken out of context could ruin a perfectly good candidate's chances of getting hired. This process could potentially give an unfair advantage to one candidate over another. It creates an unequal playing field and potentially provides hiring managers with information that can impact their hiring decision in a negative way.\nIt's hard to ignore social media as a screening tool. While there are things that you shouldn't see, there are some things that can be lawfully considered—making it a valuable source of relevant information too. Using social media screening appropriately can help ensure that you don't hire a toxic employee who will cost you money or stain your company's reputation. Consider the lawful side of this process and you may be able to hire the best employee ever. There is a delicate balance.\nScreening job candidates on social media must be done professionally and responsibly. Companies should stipulate that they will never ask for passwords, be consistent, document decisions, consider the source used and be aware that other laws may apply. In light of this, it is probably best to look later in the process and ask human resources for help in navigating it. Social media is here to stay. But before using social media to screen job candidates, consulting with management and legal teams beforehand is essential in order to comply with all laws.", "49": "Social media is absolutely everywhere. Billions of people use social media on a daily basis to create, share, and exchange ideas, messages, and information. Both individuals and businesses post regularly to engage and interact with people from around the world. It is a powerful communication medium that simultaneously provides immediate, frequent, permanent, and wide-reaching information across the globe.\nPeople post their lives on social media for the world to see. Facebook, Twitter, LinkedIn, and countless other social channels provide a quick and simple way to glimpse into a job candidate's personal life—both the positive and negative sides of it. Social media screening is tempting to use as part of the hiring process, but should employers make use of it when researching a potential candidate's background?\nIncorporating the use of social media to screen job candidates is not an uncommon practice. A 2018 survey found that almost 70% of employers use social media to screen candidates before hiring them. But there are consequences and potential legal risks involved too. When done inappropriately, social media screening can be considered unethical or even illegal.\nSocial media screening is essentially scrutinising a job candidate's private life. It can reveal information about protected characteristics like age, race, nationality, disability, gender, religion, etc., and that could bias a hiring decision. Pictures or comments on a private page that are taken out of context could ruin a perfectly good candidate's chances of getting hired. This process could potentially give an unfair advantage to one candidate over another. It creates an unequal playing field and potentially provides hiring managers with information that can impact their hiring decision in a negative way.\nIt's hard to ignore social media as a screening tool. While there are things that you shouldn't see, there are some things that can be lawfully considered—making it a valuable source of relevant information too. Using social media screening appropriately can help ensure that you don't hire a toxic employee who will cost you money or stain your company's reputation. Consider the lawful side of this process and you may be able to hire the best employee ever. There is a delicate balance.\nScreening job candidates on social media must be done professionally and responsibly. Companies should stipulate that they will never ask for passwords, be consistent, document decisions, consider the source used and be aware that other laws may apply. In light of this, it is probably best to look later in the process and ask human resources for help in navigating it. Social media is here to stay. But before using social media to screen job candidates, consulting with management and legal teams beforehand is essential in order to comply with all laws.", "50": "Social media is absolutely everywhere. Billions of people use social media on a daily basis to create, share, and exchange ideas, messages, and information. Both individuals and businesses post regularly to engage and interact with people from around the world. It is a powerful communication medium that simultaneously provides immediate, frequent, permanent, and wide-reaching information across the globe.\nPeople post their lives on social media for the world to see. Facebook, Twitter, LinkedIn, and countless other social channels provide a quick and simple way to glimpse into a job candidate's personal life—both the positive and negative sides of it. Social media screening is tempting to use as part of the hiring process, but should employers make use of it when researching a potential candidate's background?\nIncorporating the use of social media to screen job candidates is not an uncommon practice. A 2018 survey found that almost 70% of employers use social media to screen candidates before hiring them. But there are consequences and potential legal risks involved too. When done inappropriately, social media screening can be considered unethical or even illegal.\nSocial media screening is essentially scrutinising a job candidate's private life. It can reveal information about protected characteristics like age, race, nationality, disability, gender, religion, etc., and that could bias a hiring decision. Pictures or comments on a private page that are taken out of context could ruin a perfectly good candidate's chances of getting hired. This process could potentially give an unfair advantage to one candidate over another. It creates an unequal playing field and potentially provides hiring managers with information that can impact their hiring decision in a negative way.\nIt's hard to ignore social media as a screening tool. While there are things that you shouldn't see, there are some things that can be lawfully considered—making it a valuable source of relevant information too. Using social media screening appropriately can help ensure that you don't hire a toxic employee who will cost you money or stain your company's reputation. Consider the lawful side of this process and you may be able to hire the best employee ever. There is a delicate balance.\nScreening job candidates on social media must be done professionally and responsibly. Companies should stipulate that they will never ask for passwords, be consistent, document decisions, consider the source used and be aware that other laws may apply. In light of this, it is probably best to look later in the process and ask human resources for help in navigating it. Social media is here to stay. But before using social media to screen job candidates, consulting with management and legal teams beforehand is essential in order to comply with all laws.", "51": "In recent years, the food industry has increased its use of labels. Whether the labels say 'non-GMO(非转基因的)' or 'no sugar,' or 'zero carbohydrates', consumers are increasingly demanding more information about what's in their food. One report found that 39 percent of consumers would switch from the brands they currently buy to others that provide clearer, more accurate product information. Food manufacturers are responding to the report with new labels to meet that demand, and they're doing so with an eye towards giving their products an advantage over the competition, and bolstering profits.\nThis strategy makes intuitive sense. If consumers say they want transparency, tell them exactly what is in your product. That is simply supplying a certain demand. But the marketing strategy in response to this consumer demand has gone beyond articulating what is in a product, to labeling what is NOT in the food. These labels are known as \"absence claims\" labels, and they represent an emerging labeling trend that is detrimental both to the consumers who purchase the products and the industry that supplies them.\nFor example, Hunt's put a \"non-GMO\" label on its canned crushed tomatoes a few years ago—despite the fact that at the time there was no such thing as a GMO tomato on the market. Some dairy companies are using the \"non-GMO\" label on their milk, despite the fact that all milk is naturally GMO-free, another label that creates unnecessary fear around food.\nWhile creating labels that play on consumer fears and misconceptions about their food may give a company a temporary marketing advantage over competing products on the grocery aisle, in the long term this strategy will have just the opposite effect: by injecting fear into the discourse about our food, we run the risk of eroding consumer trust in not just a single product, but the entire food business.\nEventually, it becomes a question in consumers' minds: Were these foods ever safe? By purchasing and consuming these types of products, have I already done some kind of harm to my family or the planet?\nFor food manufacturers, it will mean damaged consumer trust and lower sales for everyone. And this isn't just supposition. A recent study found that absence claims labels can create a stigma around foods even when there is no scientific evidence that they cause harm.\nIt's clear that food manufacturers must tread carefully when it comes to using absence claims. In addition to the likely negative long-term impact on sales, this verbal trick sends a message that innovations in farming and food processing are unwelcome, eventually leading to less efficiency, fewer choices for consumers, and ultimately, more costly food products. If we allow this kind of labeling to continue, we will all lose.", "52": "In recent years, the food industry has increased its use of labels. Whether the labels say 'non-GMO(非转基因的)' or 'no sugar,' or 'zero carbohydrates', consumers are increasingly demanding more information about what's in their food. One report found that 39 percent of consumers would switch from the brands they currently buy to others that provide clearer, more accurate product information. Food manufacturers are responding to the report with new labels to meet that demand, and they're doing so with an eye towards giving their products an advantage over the competition, and bolstering profits.\nThis strategy makes intuitive sense. If consumers say they want transparency, tell them exactly what is in your product. That is simply supplying a certain demand. But the marketing strategy in response to this consumer demand has gone beyond articulating what is in a product, to labeling what is NOT in the food. These labels are known as \"absence claims\" labels, and they represent an emerging labeling trend that is detrimental both to the consumers who purchase the products and the industry that supplies them.\nFor example, Hunt's put a \"non-GMO\" label on its canned crushed tomatoes a few years ago—despite the fact that at the time there was no such thing as a GMO tomato on the market. Some dairy companies are using the \"non-GMO\" label on their milk, despite the fact that all milk is naturally GMO-free, another label that creates unnecessary fear around food.\nWhile creating labels that play on consumer fears and misconceptions about their food may give a company a temporary marketing advantage over competing products on the grocery aisle, in the long term this strategy will have just the opposite effect: by injecting fear into the discourse about our food, we run the risk of eroding consumer trust in not just a single product, but the entire food business.\nEventually, it becomes a question in consumers' minds: Were these foods ever safe? By purchasing and consuming these types of products, have I already done some kind of harm to my family or the planet?\nFor food manufacturers, it will mean damaged consumer trust and lower sales for everyone. And this isn't just supposition. A recent study found that absence claims labels can create a stigma around foods even when there is no scientific evidence that they cause harm.\nIt's clear that food manufacturers must tread carefully when it comes to using absence claims. In addition to the likely negative long-term impact on sales, this verbal trick sends a message that innovations in farming and food processing are unwelcome, eventually leading to less efficiency, fewer choices for consumers, and ultimately, more costly food products. If we allow this kind of labeling to continue, we will all lose.", "53": "In recent years, the food industry has increased its use of labels. Whether the labels say 'non-GMO(非转基因的)' or 'no sugar,' or 'zero carbohydrates', consumers are increasingly demanding more information about what's in their food. One report found that 39 percent of consumers would switch from the brands they currently buy to others that provide clearer, more accurate product information. Food manufacturers are responding to the report with new labels to meet that demand, and they're doing so with an eye towards giving their products an advantage over the competition, and bolstering profits.\nThis strategy makes intuitive sense. If consumers say they want transparency, tell them exactly what is in your product. That is simply supplying a certain demand. But the marketing strategy in response to this consumer demand has gone beyond articulating what is in a product, to labeling what is NOT in the food. These labels are known as \"absence claims\" labels, and they represent an emerging labeling trend that is detrimental both to the consumers who purchase the products and the industry that supplies them.\nFor example, Hunt's put a \"non-GMO\" label on its canned crushed tomatoes a few years ago—despite the fact that at the time there was no such thing as a GMO tomato on the market. Some dairy companies are using the \"non-GMO\" label on their milk, despite the fact that all milk is naturally GMO-free, another label that creates unnecessary fear around food.\nWhile creating labels that play on consumer fears and misconceptions about their food may give a company a temporary marketing advantage over competing products on the grocery aisle, in the long term this strategy will have just the opposite effect: by injecting fear into the discourse about our food, we run the risk of eroding consumer trust in not just a single product, but the entire food business.\nEventually, it becomes a question in consumers' minds: Were these foods ever safe? By purchasing and consuming these types of products, have I already done some kind of harm to my family or the planet?\nFor food manufacturers, it will mean damaged consumer trust and lower sales for everyone. And this isn't just supposition. A recent study found that absence claims labels can create a stigma around foods even when there is no scientific evidence that they cause harm.\nIt's clear that food manufacturers must tread carefully when it comes to using absence claims. In addition to the likely negative long-term impact on sales, this verbal trick sends a message that innovations in farming and food processing are unwelcome, eventually leading to less efficiency, fewer choices for consumers, and ultimately, more costly food products. If we allow this kind of labeling to continue, we will all lose.", "54": "In recent years, the food industry has increased its use of labels. Whether the labels say 'non-GMO(非转基因的)' or 'no sugar,' or 'zero carbohydrates', consumers are increasingly demanding more information about what's in their food. One report found that 39 percent of consumers would switch from the brands they currently buy to others that provide clearer, more accurate product information. Food manufacturers are responding to the report with new labels to meet that demand, and they're doing so with an eye towards giving their products an advantage over the competition, and bolstering profits.\nThis strategy makes intuitive sense. If consumers say they want transparency, tell them exactly what is in your product. That is simply supplying a certain demand. But the marketing strategy in response to this consumer demand has gone beyond articulating what is in a product, to labeling what is NOT in the food. These labels are known as \"absence claims\" labels, and they represent an emerging labeling trend that is detrimental both to the consumers who purchase the products and the industry that supplies them.\nFor example, Hunt's put a \"non-GMO\" label on its canned crushed tomatoes a few years ago—despite the fact that at the time there was no such thing as a GMO tomato on the market. Some dairy companies are using the \"non-GMO\" label on their milk, despite the fact that all milk is naturally GMO-free, another label that creates unnecessary fear around food.\nWhile creating labels that play on consumer fears and misconceptions about their food may give a company a temporary marketing advantage over competing products on the grocery aisle, in the long term this strategy will have just the opposite effect: by injecting fear into the discourse about our food, we run the risk of eroding consumer trust in not just a single product, but the entire food business.\nEventually, it becomes a question in consumers' minds: Were these foods ever safe? By purchasing and consuming these types of products, have I already done some kind of harm to my family or the planet?\nFor food manufacturers, it will mean damaged consumer trust and lower sales for everyone. And this isn't just supposition. A recent study found that absence claims labels can create a stigma around foods even when there is no scientific evidence that they cause harm.\nIt's clear that food manufacturers must tread carefully when it comes to using absence claims. In addition to the likely negative long-term impact on sales, this verbal trick sends a message that innovations in farming and food processing are unwelcome, eventually leading to less efficiency, fewer choices for consumers, and ultimately, more costly food products. If we allow this kind of labeling to continue, we will all lose.", "55": "In recent years, the food industry has increased its use of labels. Whether the labels say 'non-GMO(非转基因的)' or 'no sugar,' or 'zero carbohydrates', consumers are increasingly demanding more information about what's in their food. One report found that 39 percent of consumers would switch from the brands they currently buy to others that provide clearer, more accurate product information. Food manufacturers are responding to the report with new labels to meet that demand, and they're doing so with an eye towards giving their products an advantage over the competition, and bolstering profits.\nThis strategy makes intuitive sense. If consumers say they want transparency, tell them exactly what is in your product. That is simply supplying a certain demand. But the marketing strategy in response to this consumer demand has gone beyond articulating what is in a product, to labeling what is NOT in the food. These labels are known as \"absence claims\" labels, and they represent an emerging labeling trend that is detrimental both to the consumers who purchase the products and the industry that supplies them.\nFor example, Hunt's put a \"non-GMO\" label on its canned crushed tomatoes a few years ago—despite the fact that at the time there was no such thing as a GMO tomato on the market. Some dairy companies are using the \"non-GMO\" label on their milk, despite the fact that all milk is naturally GMO-free, another label that creates unnecessary fear around food.\nWhile creating labels that play on consumer fears and misconceptions about their food may give a company a temporary marketing advantage over competing products on the grocery aisle, in the long term this strategy will have just the opposite effect: by injecting fear into the discourse about our food, we run the risk of eroding consumer trust in not just a single product, but the entire food business.\nEventually, it becomes a question in consumers' minds: Were these foods ever safe? By purchasing and consuming these types of products, have I already done some kind of harm to my family or the planet?\nFor food manufacturers, it will mean damaged consumer trust and lower sales for everyone. And this isn't just supposition. A recent study found that absence claims labels can create a stigma around foods even when there is no scientific evidence that they cause harm.\nIt's clear that food manufacturers must tread carefully when it comes to using absence claims. In addition to the likely negative long-term impact on sales, this verbal trick sends a message that innovations in farming and food processing are unwelcome, eventually leading to less efficiency, fewer choices for consumers, and ultimately, more costly food products. If we allow this kind of labeling to continue, we will all lose."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does Sarah think David was doing for the last two weeks?", "options": ["A) He was enjoying his holiday.", "B) He was recovering in hospital.", "C) He was busy writing his essays.", "D) He was fighting a throat infection."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He was enjoying his holiday.\n【定位】We thought you had disappeared on holiday early or something.\n我们还以为你提早跑去度假了之类的呢。\n【信号】thought 引出的\"我们以为\"。答案信号是表\"猜测\"的 We thought you had disappeared\non holiday early——题干问的正是 Sarah \"think\" 什么，录音里对应的就是 thought 这\n个\"以为\"，听到\"以为/猜\"类词就锁定它是回答\"别人怎么想\"那道题的句子。\n【替换】disappeared on holiday early ↔ He was enjoying his holiday.\n去度假 = 享受假期\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 盗用了录音里 David 自述去医院的情节（had to go to the hospital），但那是\"实际发\n生\"且由 David 本人后说出来的，并非 Sarah \"以为\"的内容；题干问的是 Sarah 的猜测，故\nB 答非所问。\n借用原文：had to go to the hospital to get some antibiotics\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 盗用了录音后半 all my essays are in there（论文都在电脑里）的 essays 一词，制造\"听\n到 essays 就选\"的诱惑；但那是讲电脑坏了的麻烦，根本不是 Sarah 对\"这两周在做什么\"的\n猜测。\n借用原文：all my essays are in there\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 盗用 David 自述的 I got a throat infection last week（得了喉咙感染），是真实发生的\n病情；但题干要的是 Sarah \"以为\"的内容，Sarah 当时以为他在度假，并不知道他生病，故\nD 张冠李戴、答非所问。\n借用原文：I got a throat infection last week", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What happened to David on his way back from the hospital?", "options": ["A) He broke his wrist.", "B) He lost his antibiotics.", "C) He slipped on ice and fell.", "D) He was laughed at by some girls."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He slipped on ice and fell.\n【定位】And, on the way back from the hospital, I slipped on some ice and fell and then had to go to\nthe hospital to get an X-ray because I basically thought I broke my wrist, although,\nthankfully, it's not broken.\n而且，从医院回来的路上，我踩到冰滑倒摔了一跤，然后又得去医院拍X光，因为我当时以\n为手腕摔断了，不过谢天谢地，并没有断。\n【信号】题干同序短语 on the way back 直接对位。答案信号是录音里与题干几乎同词的 on the\nway back from the hospital——题目限定\"回来的路上\"，录音正好用同一短语领起这件\n事，听到这个时间状语就锁定其后描述的动作即答案。\n【替换】I slipped on some ice and fell ↔ He slipped on ice and fell.\n几乎原样对应的动作\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 盗用录音 I basically thought I broke my wrist 里的 broke my wrist，但紧跟着是\nalthough, thankfully, it's not broken（其实没断）。选项只取了\"以为断了\"、漏掉了否定\n反转，与原文相反，故错。\n借用原文：I basically thought I broke my wrist, although, thankfully, it's not broken\nB) 未提及\nB 盗用录音 get some antibiotics 里的 antibiotics 一词，制造熟词诱惑；但录音只说去医\n院拿抗生素，从未提\"丢了药\"，属无中生有，且与\"路上发生的事\"无关。\n借用原文：get some antibiotics\nD) 答非所问\nD 盗用录音 fall right in front of four girls 的\"被女生看到/笑\"情节，听上去确有其事；但\n那是 To make things worse 之后追加的另一桩糗事，题干问的是\"回来路上发生了什么\"，\n对应的是滑倒，D 答非所问。\n借用原文：I managed to fall right in front of four girls from the ninth grade", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does Sarah say they should do with the damaged computer?", "options": ["A) Turn to her father for help.", "B) Call the repair shop to fix it.", "C) Ask the manufacturer for repairs.", "D) Replace it with a brand-new one."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Call the repair shop to fix it.\n【定位】Listen, I have the number of a really good, affordable computer repair shop at home. My dad\nhas used this guy before and he can work miracles. Let's go back to my house and we can call\nthe repair shop.\n听着，我家里有一家很不错又实惠的电脑维修店的电话。我爸以前找过这个人，他能化腐朽\n为神奇。咱们回我家，给那家维修店打个电话吧。\n【信号】Let's… 之前被否掉、之后才是定案。前文 P7 先提\"在保修期就退给厂家换新机\"，但 P8 一\n句 The warranty ran out three days before I broke it（保修过期了）把它否掉；真正的\n定案信号是 P9 里 Sarah 的建议句 Let's go back to my house and we can call the repair\nshop——Let's 引出的才是最终方案。\n【替换】call the repair shop ↔ Call the repair shop to fix it.\n打电话给维修店 = 找维修店修\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 盗用录音 My dad has used this guy before 里的 dad，制造\"听到 dad 就选\"的诱惑；\n但录音只是说\"她爸以前找过这家维修店\"，用来佐证店靠谱，并非\"向她爸求助\"，故 A 偷换\n概念。\n借用原文：My dad has used this guy before and he can work miracles\nC) 与原文相反\nC 盗用录音 send it back to the manufacturer 里的 manufacturer，听起来像方案之一；\n但 P8 的 The warranty ran out（保修已过期）已把这条路堵死，它是被否掉的方案，非最\n终决定。\n借用原文：you can easily send it back to the manufacturer\nD) 与原文相反\nD 盗用录音 they'll send you a brand-new one 里的 brand-new，最诱人；但\"免费换新\n机\"的前提是 Is the laptop still under warranty，而保修已过期，这个方案随之作废，故 D\n与原文相反。\n借用原文：they'll send you a brand-new one for free surely", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does Sarah say she is going to do?", "options": ["A) Help David retrieve his essays.", "B) Introduce David to her parents.", "C) Offer David some refreshments.", "D) Accompany David to his home."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Offer David some refreshments.\n【定位】Let's go back to my house and we can call the repair shop, and you can have some tea and\ncookies too.\n咱们回我家，给那家维修店打个电话，你还可以喝点茶、吃点饼干。\n【信号】Let's / you can have 引出的主动招待。答案信号是 Sarah 结尾的邀约句 you can have\nsome tea and cookies too——you can have 引出她要为 David 提供的招待。听对话结\n尾热心一方 Let's…、you can… 的邀约动作，就是\"她要做什么\"的落点。\n【替换】you can have some tea and cookies too ↔ Offer David some refreshments.\n茶和饼干 = refreshments（茶点）\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 盗用录音 all my essays are in there 的 essays，听上去像\"帮他取回论文\"；但 Sarah 提\n供的是\"打电话找维修店修电脑\"，retrieve essays（取回论文）是修好后才可能的结果，录\n音并未说她要去取论文，属过度推断。\n借用原文：all my essays are in there\nB) 未提及\nB 借\"去她家、提到她 dad\"的语境（My dad has used this guy before）让人联想\"见家\n长\"；但录音只说回她家打电话、吃茶点，从没提\"introduce David to her parents（引见\n给父母）\"，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 把录音 Let's go back to my house（回我家）的方向偷偷反过来，写成 Accompany\nDavid to his home.（陪他回他家）。go back to my house 指\"去 Sarah 家\"，与\"to his\nhome\"正好相反，属方向/地点偷换。\n借用原文：Let's go back to my house", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man say about the woman?", "options": ["A) She is a critic of works on military affairs.", "B) She is an acclaimed hostess of Book Talk.", "C) She is a researcher of literary genres.", "D) She is a historian of military history."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is a historian of military history.\n【定位】With me today is Heidi Brown, a historian who has written five critically acclaimed books\nabout military history.\n今天与我同台的是海蒂·布朗，一位写过五本广受好评的军事史著作的历史学家。\n【信号】主持人开场介绍嘉宾的应答位。信号是开场白里主持人介绍来宾的那句 With me today is\nHeidi Brown, a historian…——访谈类对话，嘉宾身份就在主持人 \"今天的嘉宾是…\" 这句\n里点出，这是身份题的固定落点。\n【替换】a historian who has written five critically acclaimed books about military history ↔\nShe is a historian of military history.\n把长定语压缩成 historian of military history\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ncritic of works on military affairs 借了原文的 military 一词，但原文说她 has written\nfive … books（她是写书的作者），不是 \"评论他人作品的评论家\"，身份对不上。\n借用原文：has written five critically acclaimed books about military history\nB) 张冠李戴\nhostess of Book Talk 盗用了开场的节目名 Book Talk，但主持节目的是男方（he），女方\n是受邀嘉宾，把主持人身份安到了女方头上。\n借用原文：Welcome to this week's episode of Book Talk\nC) 偷换概念\nresearcher of literary genres 里的 genres 蹭了后文谈体裁的内容，但原文明确说她是 a\nhistorian，不是 \"文学体裁研究者\"，身份被偷换。\n借用原文：your first attempt at that genre", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman say about her newly published book?", "options": ["A) It is about the military history of Europe.", "B) It is set in the 18th and 19th centuries.", "C) It is her fifth book of military history.", "D) It is a war novel set in the future."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is a war novel set in the future.\n【定位】I'd say it's a major departure as it's not just a work of fiction. It's set 200 years in the future.\n我会说这是一次重大转变，因为它不只是一部虚构作品，它的背景设定在两百年后的未来。\n【信号】被问者亲口描述新书的应答句。信号是女方自己介绍新书的那句 It's set 200 years in the\nfuture——题目问 \"woman say about her book\"，落点就在被问者亲口说书的特征处；\"a\nwork of fiction\"（虚构作品）+ \"set in the future\"（设定在未来）两点合起来，就是一部\n\"设定在未来的战争小说\"。\n【替换】a work of fiction ↔ a war novel\nfiction=novel，结合前文军人题材即 war novel\nIt's set 200 years in the future ↔ set in the future\n200 years in the future=in the future\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nmilitary history of Europe 蹭了开场说她写 military history 的话，但那指她过去五本书，\n新书是面向未来的小说；且原文从未提 Europe。\n借用原文：five critically acclaimed books about military history\nB) 与原文相反\nset in the 18th and 19th centuries 直接盗用主持人那句 focusing on the 18th and 19th\ncenturies，但那说的是她 \"过去三十年研究的年代\"，新书恰恰相反，是设定在 future。\n借用原文：focusing on the 18th and 19th centuries\nC) 原词陷阱\nher fifth book of military history 盗用了 five … books about military history 里的\nfive，但 five 是她此前史书的数量，新书是新出的小说，不是 \"第五本军事史\"。\n借用原文：has written five critically acclaimed books about military history", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What did the woman do before writing her new book?", "options": ["A) She visited soldiers' wives and mothers.", "B) She conducted surveys of many soldiers.", "C) She met a large number of soldiers in person.", "D) She looked into the personal lives of soldiers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She looked into the personal lives of soldiers.\n【定位】That focus came about when I still intended to write another book of history. I started by\nresearching soldiers' actual personal lives, studying their letters home.\n这个聚焦点是在我还打算再写一本史书时形成的。我先是研究士兵真实的私人生活，研读他\n们写回家的信。\n【信号】被问者讲自己怎么起步的应答句。信号是女方自述研究起点的那句 I started by\nresearching soldiers' actual personal lives——题目问她写书前做了什么，落点就在被\n问者讲自己研究动作的句子；\"researching … personal lives\"（研究私人生活）就是答案\n动作。\n【替换】researching soldiers' actual personal lives ↔ looked into the personal lives of soldiers\nresearching=looked into，词序调整\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nvisited soldiers' wives and mothers 盗用第7段 the bond between sons and mothers\nand men and their wives 里的 mothers、wives，但那说的是小说写的关系，不是她去\n\"探访\" 了这些人，动作无中生有。\n借用原文：the bond between sons and mothers and men and their wives\nB) 偷换概念\nconducted surveys of many soldiers 与原文 studying their letters home 方式不符——\n她是研读士兵的家书，不是 \"对众多士兵做问卷调查\"，调研方式被偷换。\n借用原文：studying their letters home\nC) 原词陷阱\nmet a large number of soldiers in person 借了原文反复出现的 soldiers，但她是\nstudying their letters home（读信），原文从未说她 \"当面见了大量士兵\"，in person 与\n数量都是加出来的。\n借用原文：after years of researching soldiers and chronicling their lives", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman say about her writing history books?", "options": ["A) She doesn't have much freedom for imagination.", "B) It is not easy to make her readers believe in her.", "C) It is difficult to attract young readers.", "D) She has to combine fact with fiction."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） She doesn't have much freedom for imagination.\n【定位】I felt too constrained working with the past, like what I wrote needed to be fact as opposed to\nfiction, but writing about the future gave me more freedom to imagine, to invent.\n处理过去的题材让我感到太受束缚，好像我写的东西必须是事实而不能是虚构；而写未来则\n给了我更多想象、虚构的自由。\n【信号】感受词 felt too constrained + 转折 but。信号有两处：一是被问者直陈感受的 I felt too\nconstrained working with the past（写过去让我太受束缚），二是 but writing about\nthe future gave me more freedom 的转折 but——\"过去受限 / 未来更自由\" 的对比正是\n答案落点；写史书（过去）即 \"没有多少想象的自由\"。\n【替换】I felt too constrained working with the past ↔ She doesn't have much freedom for imagination.\nconstrained=没有多少自由，肯否定转换\nwriting about the future gave me more freedom to imagine, to invent ↔ freedom for imagination\nfreedom to imagine=freedom for imagination\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nmake her readers believe in her 蹭了主持人那句 any historical novel you write would\nhave a lot of credibility 的 credibility，但那是主持人讲 \"你写历史小说会很可信\"，不是她\n说 \"难让读者相信她\"，张冠李戴且意思相反。\n借用原文：any historical novel you write would have a lot of credibility\nC) 未提及\ndifficult to attract young readers 里的 \"young readers\" 原文从头到尾没出现，她谈的是\n写作自由问题，跟 \"吸引年轻读者\" 毫无关系。\nD) 与原文相反\ncombine fact with fiction 直接搬来原文的 fact as opposed to fiction，但原文是 \"事实\n与 虚构 相对立\"，且她觉得写史书时只能要 fact 不能要 fiction，根本不是 \"把事实与虚构\n结合\"。\n借用原文：what I wrote needed to be fact as opposed to fiction", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the passage say appears in almost all ads for Coca-Cola?", "options": ["A) Santa Claus.", "B) A polar bear.", "C) Cocoa seeds.", "D) A glass bottle."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） A glass bottle.\n【定位】there's one thing you'll find in nearly all ads for Coca-Cola: the characteristic glass bottle.\n在几乎所有可口可乐的广告里，你都会发现一样东西：那个标志性的玻璃瓶。\n【信号】范围词 nearly all 锁定唯一答案。答案句用 one thing you'll find in nearly all ads（在几\n乎所有广告里都能找到的\"一样东西\"），\"one thing\"\"nearly all\" 这种唯一性加全范围的\n限定，正是答案信号——它把临时露脸的圣诞老人、北极熊排除，只指向冒号后的那个玻\n璃瓶。\n【替换】the characteristic glass bottle ↔ A glass bottle.\n去掉修饰词的同一物\nin nearly all ads for Coca-Cola ↔ in almost all ads for Coca-Cola\nnearly all 等于 almost all\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念了 Santa Claus，但 Whether it's in the hands of animated polar bears or\nSanta Claus 是在说\"不管是在北极熊还是圣诞老人手里\"，他俩只是举例，真正\"几乎所有广\n告都有\"的是后面那个玻璃瓶，不是圣诞老人。\n借用原文：Whether it's in the hands of animated polar bears or Santa Claus\nB) 原词陷阱\nA polar bear. 来自 in the hands of animated polar bears，和圣诞老人并列做举例，\n是\"拿着瓶子的角色\"而非\"几乎所有广告都有的那样东西\"，听到原词就选会掉坑。\n借用原文：animated polar bears\nC) 答非所问\n可可豆出现在第3段讲设计灵感时，landing on cocoa seed，说的是瓶子\"按可可豆形状设\n计\"，并不是\"出现在广告里\"的东西，与题干问的 ads 错位。\n借用原文：landing on cocoa seed", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Why did the Coca-Cola Company decide to have special packaging designed?", "options": ["A) To attract customer attention.", "B) To keep up with the times.", "C) To combat counterfeits.", "D) To promote its sales."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To combat counterfeits.\n【定位】As the soft drink gained in popularity, it faced a growing number of competitors—\ncounterfeits even trying to copy Coke's logo. So, according to Coca-Cola historian Ted Ryan,\nthe company decided to come up with packaging that couldn't be duplicated.\n随着这款软饮越来越受欢迎，它面对的竞争者越来越多——甚至有仿冒品试图抄袭可口可\n乐的logo。于是，据可口可乐历史学家泰德·瑞安所说，公司决定设计一种无法被复制的\n包装。\n【信号】因果信号 So 连接原因与决定。counterfeits even trying to copy Coke's logo. So…the\ncompany decided to come up with packaging that couldn't be duplicated，一个 So\n把前因（被仿冒、logo被抄）和后果（决定设计不可复制的包装）连起来，So 前那句就\n是\"为什么设计特殊包装\"的答案信号。\n【替换】counterfeits even trying to copy Coke's logo ↔ To combat counterfeits.\n应对仿冒即打击仿冒\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTo attract customer attention.（吸引顾客注意）听上去对任何包装设计都成立，但录音从\n头到尾没说为了\"吸引注意\"，给出的原因只有\"防仿冒\"，这是凭常识脑补出来的，不是原文\n给的。\nB) 未提及\nTo keep up with the times.（跟上时代）在录音里没有任何对应，原文谈的是\"被仿冒所以\n要做防复制包装\"，跟\"时代\"无关，是凭空添加的理由。\nD) 答非所问\nTo promote its sales.（促进销售）虽合常识，但录音里\"设计特殊包装\"这个决定的直接原\n因是 So 前那句\"被仿冒\"，不是为了促销。销量大增（Coke bottle sales had ballooned\ninto billions）是结果，不是设计包装的初衷，时间和因果都对不上。\n借用原文：Coke bottle sales had ballooned into billions", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What do we learn about the Coca-Cola bottle designed by the Root Glass Company?", "options": ["A) It resembles a picture in the encyclopedia.", "B) It appears in the shape of a cocoa seed.", "C) It has the drink's logo in the middle.", "D) It displays the image of Santa Claus."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It appears in the shape of a cocoa seed.\n【定位】Workers at the Root Glass Company got the request and began flipping through the\nencyclopedia at the local library, landing on cocoa seed. Though cocoa seed is not an\ningredient of the soda, they designed their bottle based on the seed's shape and large middle.\nRoot玻璃公司的工人接到这个需求，开始在当地图书馆翻百科全书，最后翻到了可可豆。尽\n管可可豆并不是这款软饮的成分，他们还是按照可可豆的形状和宽大的中部设计了瓶子。\n【信号】designed…based on 直陈设计依据。they designed their bottle based on the seed's\nshape and large middle，designed…based on（按照……来设计）是直接交代瓶子\"长\n什么样、依什么而来\"的核心句，正是瓶子特征的答案信号。其中 seed 指上句翻到的\ncocoa seed。\n【替换】designed their bottle based on the seed's shape and large middle ↔\nIt appears in the shape of a cocoa seed.\n按可可豆形状设计=呈可可豆形状\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念了 encyclopedia，began flipping through the encyclopedia 说的是工人\"翻\n百科全书\"才找到可可豆，百科全书只是查找的途径；选项 It resembles a picture in the\nencyclopedia. 却说瓶子\"像百科里的一幅图\"，把\"在百科里查到灵感\"偷换成\"瓶子长得像百\n科图片\"，原文没这么说。\n借用原文：began flipping through the encyclopedia at the local library\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt has the drink's logo in the middle. 把第2段的 logo 和本段的 large middle 拼在一\n起，貌似有据。但 logo 出现在 counterfeits even trying to copy Coke's logo（讲仿冒抄\n袭logo），原文从没说瓶子\"中间有logo\"；瓶子的中部是 large middle（宽大的中部），\n与\"有logo\"无关。\n借用原文：trying to copy Coke's logo\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt displays the image of Santa Claus. 搬来第1段的 Santa Claus，但圣诞老人出现在 in\nthe hands of…Santa Claus（广告里的形象），原文从未说瓶子上\"显示圣诞老人图像\"，这\n是把广告形象错安到瓶子身上。\n借用原文：in the hands of animated polar bears or Santa Claus", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does research show about a conversation between strangers?", "options": ["A) It often occurs among commuters.", "B) It promotes mutual understanding.", "C) It improves their mood considerably.", "D) It takes a great deal of effort to sustain."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It improves their mood considerably.\n【定位】Research shows that a few moments of conversation with a stranger creates a measurable\nimprovement in mood.\n研究表明，与陌生人交谈片刻就能让心情产生可测量的改善。\n【信号】Research shows 引出结论。句首 Research shows that 是明确的结论信号，紧跟其后的\ncreates a measurable improvement in mood 就是研究的核心发现，正对题干的\nresearch show。\n【替换】creates a measurable improvement in mood ↔ improves their mood considerably\ncreates improvement = improves\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt often occurs among commuters. 借了录音第2段的 commuters 一词制造耳熟，但研\n究的结论是交谈改善心情，并非说交谈'常发生在通勤者中间'，原词陷阱。\n借用原文：commuters who talked to nearby strangers\nB) 未提及\nIt promotes mutual understanding. 听起来很合理，但录音通篇讲的是改善心情\n（mood）、让人更愉快，从未说交谈'促进相互理解'，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt takes a great deal of effort to sustain. 说交谈很费力，而录音说的是 a few moments\nof conversation 就能改善心情，强调轻松易得，方向相反。\n借用原文：a few moments of conversation with a stranger", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What prevents people from starting conversation with strangers?", "options": ["A) Social anxiety.", "B) Excessive caution.", "C) Lack of social skills.", "D) Preference for solitude."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Social anxiety.\n【定位】Social anxiety appears to be the problem.\n社交焦虑似乎就是症结所在。\n【信号】appears to be the problem 点名症结。appears to be the problem 直接把'问题所在'点\n出来，这正是题干 prevents（阻碍）对应的那个原因，是答案句的明确信号。\n【替换】Social anxiety appears to be the problem ↔ Social anxiety\n原词直接对应\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nExcessive caution. 看似与社交焦虑相近，但录音从未用 caution 或'过度谨慎'来解释，把\n焦虑悄悄换成谨慎，属偷换概念。\nC) 未提及\nLack of social skills. 听上去也像不愿交谈的理由，但录音解释的是人们 underestimating\nothers' interest（低估别人交谈意愿），不是缺技巧，无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\nPreference for solitude. 借了第2段 the more solitary experience 的耳熟感，但那是说通\n勤者'预期'独处更愉快，并非说人们因'偏爱独处'而不交谈，盗用原词又偷换。\n借用原文：most expected the more solitary experience to be more pleasurable", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "Why does a negative encounter with strangers stick in one's memory?", "options": ["A) People usually regard it as an unforgettable lesson.", "B) Human brains tend to dwell on negative events.", "C) Negative events often hurt people deeply.", "D) People generally resent being rejected."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Human brains tend to dwell on negative events.\n【定位】You may occasionally have a negative encounter that might stick in your memory. This is\nbecause the human brain is biased to dwell on negative events.\n你偶尔会遇到不愉快的经历，可能会留在记忆里。这是因为人脑天生偏向于反复回想负面\n事件。\n【信号】This is because 因果信号。This is because 是显眼的因果连接词，承上句的 stick in your\nmemory，下接真正原因 the human brain is biased to dwell on negative events，正是\n答案信号。\n【替换】the human brain is biased to dwell on negative events ↔\nHuman brains tend to dwell on negative events\nis biased to = tend to\n【排除】A) 未提及\nPeople usually regard it as an unforgettable lesson. 把'记住'解释成'当作难忘教训'，但\n录音给的原因是大脑机制，从未说人们把它当教训，无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\nNegative events often hurt people deeply. 借了 negative 的耳熟感，说负面事件'伤人\n很深'，但题干问的是'为何留在记忆'，原因是大脑偏向回想、不是伤得深，答非所问。\n借用原文：dwell on negative events\nD) 原词陷阱\nPeople generally resent being rejected. 借了第4段末 the risk of rejection 的耳熟感，但\n那是另一句话，且'怨恨被拒'并不解释'为何记住负面经历'，原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the risk of rejection", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the passage say the pleasure of connection seems to be?", "options": ["A) Contagious.", "B) Temporary.", "C) Unpredictable.", "D) Measurable."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Contagious.\n【定位】The pleasure of connection seems contagious.\n连接的愉悦似乎是会传染的。\n【信号】seems 后形容词即答案。The pleasure of connection seems contagious 与题干\npleasure of connection seems to be 几乎逐词对应，seems 后的形容词 contagious 就\n是答案信号。\n【替换】seems contagious ↔ Contagious\n形容词原词对应\n【排除】B) 未提及\nTemporary. 录音从未说这种愉悦是'短暂'的，无中生有，且与全文鼓励交谈、强调正面的基\n调不符。\nC) 未提及 Unpredictable. 录音没有任何关于'难以预测'的说法，纯属无中生有。\nD) 张冠李戴\nMeasurable. 借了第1段 a measurable improvement in mood 的耳熟感，但\nmeasurable 是形容第1段的'心情改善'，不是第5段的'连接愉悦'，把别处的修饰词安到本题\n主语上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a measurable improvement in mood", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the economy of the Caribbean islands?", "options": ["A) It depends heavily on tourism.", "B) It is flourishing in foreign trade.", "C) It is mainly based on agriculture.", "D) It relies chiefly on mineral export."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is mainly based on agriculture.\n【定位】This tropical region's economy is based mainly on farming.\n这个热带地区的经济主要建立在农业（耕作）之上。\n【信号】based mainly on 正面给定。答案信号是 economy is based mainly on farming 里的\nmainly——「主要」二字直接把经济支柱钉在 farming 上，这种「主语 + based mainly\non + X」是篇章里下结论的典型句式，听到 mainly 就锁定。\n【替换】based mainly on farming ↔ mainly based on agriculture\nfarming=agriculture 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第三段确实大谈 tourism has increased rapidly，A 把这个真出现过的 tourism 拎出\n来冒充经济支柱，但讲经济基础的第一段明说是 farming，旅游只是后文另起的话题。\n借用原文：tourism has increased rapidly\nB) 未提及\n录音通篇没说经济「靠外贸 / 外贸繁荣」，第二段虽列了 export crops，但那是讲「出口哪\n些农产品」，不是说「经济建立在对外贸易上」，B 属于无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音第二段提到 From the West Indies also come oil, mineral pitch、铝矿等矿产，D 抓\n住这个真出现的 mineral 说经济「主要靠矿产出口」，但原文用 also（还有）把它列为次\n要物产，主轴明说是 farming。\n借用原文：From the West Indies also come oil, mineral pitch", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is the main product of the Caribbean islands?", "options": ["A) Tobacco.", "B) Bananas.", "C) Coffee.", "D) Sugar."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Sugar.\n【定位】Sugar is the main product.\n糖是主要产品。\n【信号】the main product 唯一性标志。信号是 Sugar is the main product 里的 the main——\n这是一句干净利落的判断句，直接把「主要产品」的头衔给了 sugar；篇章里这种「X is\nthe main / the only …」就是答案句的标准长相。\n【替换】Sugar is the main product ↔ Sugar.\n原词同指即答案\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 Other export crops are tobacco, coffee, bananas 把 tobacco 列在「其他出口作\n物」里，A 抓住这个真念到的 tobacco，但它前面写着 other（其他的），明摆着是配角，\n不是 main。\n借用原文：Other export crops are tobacco, coffee, bananas\nB) 原词陷阱\nbananas 同样出自 Other export crops are tobacco, coffee, bananas，与烟草、咖啡一\n道属于「其他作物」，被念到不等于是主产品。\n借用原文：Other export crops are tobacco, coffee, bananas\nC) 原词陷阱\ncoffee 也来自 tobacco, coffee, bananas 这串「其他出口作物」，与答案句的 the main\nproduct 无关，听到就选即落坑。\n借用原文：tobacco, coffee, bananas", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do we learn about the majority of people in the Caribbean islands?", "options": ["A) They toil on farms.", "B) They live a poor life.", "C) They live in Spanish-style houses.", "D) They hire people to do housework."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They live a poor life.\n【定位】But here the poor greatly outnumber the wealthy. ... Most of the people live well below the\npoverty level.\n但在这里，穷人远远多于富人。……大多数人的生活水平远在贫困线以下。\n【信号】转折 But + 数量词点群。双重信号：But here the poor greatly outnumber the wealthy\n用转折 But 把重心从前句的富人扭向穷人，再用 Most of the people live well below the\npoverty level 的 Most 把「贫困」正式派给大多数人——转折后 + most 出现处，就是答\n案落点。\n【替换】Most of the people live well below the poverty level ↔ They live a poor life.\nbelow poverty=poor 概括\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n录音第一段说 the small cultivator is working only a few acres of land，A「在农场劳\n作」沾了「耕作」的边，但那是讲农民这一小群，第四段描述多数穷人时讲的是 they live\ncrowded together in tiny houses（挤在小屋里），并未说多数人都在田里劳作。\n借用原文：the small cultivator is working only a few acres of land\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音 A visitor will find rich people living in apartments or Spanish houses 明说住西班\n牙式房子的是 rich people（富人），C 把这句富人的真实细节张冠李戴到「多数人」头\n上，正好与题问的 majority 相反。\n借用原文：rich people living in apartments or Spanish houses\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音 Their servants might include a cook, a maid, and a nurse 说请仆人的是前句的\nrich people，D「雇人做家务」盗用了这处真出现的 servants 描写，却同样安错了对象\n——是少数富人，不是多数人。\n借用原文：Their servants might include a cook, a maid, and a nurse", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What will happen to children if they always get immediate satisfaction?", "options": ["A) They will be more demanding of their next generation.", "B) They will end up lonely, dependent and dissatisfied.", "C) They will experience more setbacks than successes.", "D) They will find it difficult to get along with others."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They will end up lonely, dependent and dissatisfied.\n【定位】But in the longer term, such children end up lonely, dependent, chronically dissatisfied and\nresentful of the parents who tried so hard to please them.\n但从长远看，这样的孩子最终会变得孤独、依赖、长期不满，并怨恨那些拼命想取悦他们的\n父母。\n【信号】转折 But + 结果词 end up。这句以 But in the longer term 起头，转折信号 But 把前\n面\"短期或许是对的\"翻转，后半才是真正结论；再加 such children end up 的 end up 是\n明确的结果信号，双重提示这就是答案句。\n【替换】such children end up lonely, dependent, chronically dissatisfied ↔\nThey will end up lonely, dependent and dissatisfied.\n近乎照搬但删了 chronically\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 demanding, selfish and spoiled children 里的 demanding，A 用 more\ndemanding of their next generation 把这个原词搬来，但录音说的是孩子本身\"难缠\"，不\n是说他们将来\"对下一代更苛求\"，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：demanding, selfish and spoiled children\nC) 未提及\nC 说孩子会经历 more setbacks than successes，但录音第1段讲长远后果时只提孤独、依\n赖、不满，从未说\"挫折多于成功\"，这是录音未提及的内容。\nD) 过度推断\nD 的 difficult to get along with others 看似贴近\"孤独\"，但录音长远后果那句只说 lonely\n/ dependent / dissatisfied / resentful，并未说\"难以与人相处\"，是从 lonely 过度推断出\n来的，原话没这层意思。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What may prevent parents from raising confident and capable children?", "options": ["A) Failure to pay due attention to their behavior.", "B) Unwillingness to allow them to play with toys.", "C) Unwillingness to satisfy their wishes immediately.", "D) Failure to spend sufficient quality time with them."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Failure to spend sufficient quality time with them.\n【定位】One factor hindering this is that parents can't or don't spend enough quality time with their\nkids, and substitute this deficit with toys, games, gadgets and the like.\n妨碍这一点的一个因素是，父母不能或不愿花足够的高质量时间陪孩子，转而用玩具、游\n戏、电子产品之类来填补这一缺口。\n【信号】因果信号 One factor hindering。这句以 One factor hindering this is that 开头，\nhindering（妨碍）直接呼应题干的 prevent，是明确的\"原因信号\"，紧随其后的内容就是\n要找的妨碍因素。\n【替换】parents can't or don't spend enough quality time with their kids ↔\nFailure to spend sufficient quality time with them.\ncan't or don't＝Failure，enough＝sufficient\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说孩子需要 parents' devoted attention，A 用 Failure to pay due attention to their\nbehavior 把 attention 这个原词搬来，却拼成\"不关注孩子的行为\"，而原话讲的是\"投入式\n的陪伴关注\"，不是关注 behavior，属盗用原词后偷换对象。\n借用原文：children need parents' devoted attention\nB) 与原文相反\n录音里 substitute this deficit with toys, games, gadgets 把 toys 当作父母\"拿来替代陪\n伴\"的东西，B 却说成 Unwillingness to allow them to play with toys（不让孩子玩玩\n具），与原话意思相反，是盗用 toys 后反着说。\n借用原文：substitute this deficit with toys, games, gadgets and the like\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 的 satisfy their wishes immediately 借用了录音 instantly satisfying their wishes 里\n的\"立刻满足\"，但那是第19/21题的话题、是\"父母不该做的事\"，并不是本题问的\"妨碍培养\n自信能干孩子\"的因素，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Instead of instantly satisfying their wishes", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "Why should children be encouraged to keep trying when they fail?", "options": ["A) It will enable them to learn from mistakes.", "B) It will help them to handle disappointment.", "C) It will do much good to their mental health.", "D) It will build their ability to endure hardships"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It will help them to handle disappointment.\n【定位】This teaches them how to handle and recover from disappointment, which is associated with\ngreater success and satisfaction academically, financially and in personal relationships.\n这教会他们如何应对失望并从中恢复，而这又与学业、经济和人际关系上更大的成功与满足\n相关联。\n【信号】作用信号 This teaches them。这句以 This teaches them how to handle and recover\nfrom disappointment 开头，This teaches them 是明确的\"作用/理由\"信号，直接回答题\n干的 Why，后半的内容就是\"坚持尝试\"带来的好处。\n【替换】how to handle and recover from disappointment ↔ It will help them to handle disappointment.\nhandle disappointment 原义保留\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说 learn from mistakes，听起来很符合\"失败时坚持\"的常识，但录音第4段讲的是\nhandle and recover from disappointment，并没有说\"从错误中学习\"，是凭印象补出来\n的内容。\nC) 过度推断\nC 的 good to their mental health 看似贴近\"更健康\"，但录音说的是 greater success\nand satisfaction academically, financially and in personal relationships，讲的是成功与\n满足，并未说\"有益心理健康\"，是从相邻段 healthier 过度推断而来。\n借用原文：greater success and satisfaction academically, financially and in personal relationships\nD) 偷换概念\nD 的 endure hardships（忍受艰难）与 B 的\"应对失望\"语义相近、最易混，但录音原话明\n确是 handle and recover from disappointment，是\"应对失望\"而非\"忍受艰苦\"，D 是凭\n近义自造的说法。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say can easily prevent an interviewee from getting a job?", "options": ["A) Failing to make sufficient preparations.", "B) Looking away from the hiring manager.", "C) Saying the wrong thing at the wrong time.", "D) Making a wrong judgment of the interview."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Saying the wrong thing at the wrong time.\n【定位】And your chances of being more carefully considered for the job can quickly go downhill just\nby saying the wrong thing at the wrong time.\n而仅仅因为在错误的时间说了错误的话，你被认真考虑录用的机会就会迅速下滑。\n【信号】坏结果信号 go downhill。can quickly go downhill 是\"机会迅速变差\"的坏结果信号，它\n后面紧跟的 just by saying the wrong thing at the wrong time 正是造成坏结果的行为，\n这句因此是答案句。\n【替换】saying the wrong thing at the wrong time ↔ Saying the wrong thing at the wrong time\n原句直接成项\n【排除】A) 未提及\nFailing to make sufficient preparations. 准备不足是常识里\"会面试失败\"的原因，听起来\n合理，但录音第一段只说\"说错话\"会让机会下滑，从没提\"准备\"，属凭常识想当然。\nB) 原词陷阱\nLooking away from the hiring manager. 借了第一段真出现过的 hiring manager 一词，\n让人听到耳熟就想选，但录音说的是坐在招聘经理对面会紧张，从没说\"移开视线\"会害你丢\n工作。\n借用原文：sitting across from a hiring manager\nD) 偷换概念\nMaking a wrong judgment of the interview. 用了录音里的 judgment，但原文 open\nthemselves up for judgment 指\"面试者被别人评判\"，不是\"面试者自己判断失误\"，对象\n正好反了。\n借用原文：answering questions that effectively open themselves up for judgment", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What should the interviewee avoid doing in an interview?", "options": ["A) Complaining about their previous job.", "B) Inquiring about their salary to be paid.", "C) Exaggerating their academic background.", "D) Understating their previous achievements."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Complaining about their previous job.\n【定位】The most obvious thing not to do is complain. Employers want to hire positive people.\nTalking about a previous job negatively raises concerns that you might be difficult to manage.\n最不该做的一件事就是抱怨。雇主想招积极的人。负面地谈论上一份工作会让人担心你可能\n不好管理。\n【信号】禁止信号 thing not to do。The most obvious thing not to do is complain 用 not to\ndo 这一最强禁止信号直接点名 complain，是本题答案句；后文 Talking about a previous\njob negatively 进一步把\"抱怨\"具体化为\"负面谈上一份工作\"。\n【替换】Talking about a previous job negatively ↔ Complaining about their previous job\nnegatively=complaining 同义替换\n【排除】B) 未提及\nInquiring about their salary to be paid. 打听薪水常被视为面试敏感举动、很像\"该避\n免\"，但录音第二段只说别抱怨，全篇没提薪水，是凭常识硬塞的答案。\nC) 未提及\nExaggerating their academic background. 夸大学历是面试禁忌的常见说法，听着也对，\n但录音里没有任何关于\"学历\"或\"夸大\"的内容，属未提及。\nD) 与原文相反\nUnderstating their previous achievements. 说\"贬低自己以往成绩\"。录音第四段其实是\n叫人别把功劳全揽到自己头上、要归功团队 never take all the credit for\naccomplishments，那是\"别独揽功劳\"，与\"低估自己成绩\"方向相反，也不是本题第二段的\n内容。\n借用原文：never take all the credit for accomplishments or achievements", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What kind of employees do companies like to recruit?", "options": ["A) Those who have both skill and experience.", "B) Those who get along well with colleagues.", "C) Those who take initiative in their work.", "D) Those who are loyal to their managers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Those who take initiative in their work.\n【定位】Managers usually love people who can self-sustain and enable growth through taking\ninitiative, who are strong at following through their work, and who bring ideas and solutions\nto the table.\n经理们通常喜欢那些能够自我驱动、通过主动作为来推动成长的人，那些能把工作贯彻到底\n的人，以及那些能带来想法和解决方案的人。\n【信号】偏好信号 love people who。Managers usually love people who 中的 love 是\"喜欢哪\n种人\"的偏好信号，紧跟其后的 taking initiative 等特质就是公司想要的，是本题答案句。\n【替换】enable growth through taking initiative ↔ take initiative in their work\ntaking initiative 近乎原义对应\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nThose who have both skill and experience.\"既有技能又有经验\"听着是公司最想要的全能\n人才，很诱人，但本段说的是\"主动、把活做到底、带方案\"，并没说\"技能+经验\"；skill 一\n词要到第五段讲小公司时才出现，与本题问的\"公司喜欢哪种人\"不在同一处。\nB) 未提及\nThose who get along well with colleagues.\"与同事相处融洽\"是大家公认的好特质，但录\n音第三段夸的是主动、能跟进、带方案，从没提\"与同事关系\"，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nThose who are loyal to their managers. 借了本段反复出现的 manager 一词，又把\"忠\n诚\"包装成美德，极易误选；但录音说的是\"经理喜欢主动的人\"，没有任何\"对经理忠诚\"的意\n思。\n借用原文：Managers usually love people who can self-sustain", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is especially important for those working in a small company?", "options": ["A) Ability to shoulder new responsibilities.", "B) Experience of performing multiple roles.", "C) Readiness to work to flexible schedules.", "D) Skills to communicate with colleagues."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Ability to shoulder new responsibilities.\n【定位】This is especially true in small companies, where the ability to adapt and take on new\nresponsibilities is highly valued.\n这在小公司里尤其如此，在那里，适应并承担新职责的能力备受看重。\n【信号】强调信号 especially true + highly valued。This is especially true in small companies\n把场景锁定到小公司，is highly valued 是\"备受看重\"的强调信号，二者之间的 take on\nnew responsibilities 正是被看重的能力，这句是答案句。\n【替换】take on new responsibilities ↔ shoulder new responsibilities\ntake on=shoulder 同义替换\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nExperience of performing multiple roles. 由录音 your role is whatever you make of\nit\"角色由你定\"顺势联想出\"做过多种角色的经验\"，看似沾边，但原文强调的是\"愿意承担新\n责、能适应\"，不是\"已有多角色经验\"，是从原话多推了一步。\n借用原文：your role is whatever you make of it\nC) 原词陷阱\nReadiness to work to flexible schedules. 借了录音 you're flexible 的 flexible 一词，让\n听到\"灵活\"就想选；但原文的 flexible 指\"愿意承担超出本职的工作\"，不是\"弹性的上班时间\n表\"，对象被偷换了。\n借用原文：try and convey the idea that you're flexible\nD) 未提及\nSkills to communicate with colleagues. 沟通能力是公认的职场好技能，但第五段讲小公\n司时说的是适应与担新责，并未提\"与同事沟通\"，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) beyond\n副词\n【词性槽】and 后并列数字 85 的修饰成分\n【依据】beyond 表\"以上、超过\"，正好把 85 岁再往上推\nlive to age 85 or beyond 是英文里常见表达，意为\"活到 85 岁或以上\"，与上文 far more\nlikely to live to age 85 or __(26)__ 想强调的\"长寿\"主题完全契合。\n【竞争词】F) henceforth 时间副词，与年龄数字并列不通\nhenceforth 意为\"今后、自此\"，是时间起点副词，与 to age 85 无法构成\"85 或更高\"这种数值\n并列。\nI) plausibly 情态副词，没修饰数字的用法\nplausibly 表\"貌似可信地\"，通常修饰陈述或动词，没有 age 85 or plausibly 的搭配。\nM) specifically 义为\"具体来说\"，不表数值范围\nspecifically 用于举例或限定具体范围，无法与\"to age 85\"并列出\"85 或以上\"的含义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·and 后并列数字 85 的修饰成分"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) noteworthy\n形容词\n【词性槽】It is __ that 形式主语句的表语\n【依据】强调\"研究发现值得关注\"，与下文一长串排除变量呼应\n后文 the findings held even after the researchers considered factors that could\n__(28)__ the link, including whether participants had health conditions such as\nheart disease or cancer, or whether they experienced depression. 说研究人员排\n除了多种干扰因素后结论依然成立——这种排除后依然站得住的发现，正是\"值得注\n意的\"（noteworthy）。\n【竞争词】G) lofty 义为\"崇高的、高远的\"，多修饰理想/目标\nlofty ideals/goals 才是典型搭配，描述\"研究发现\"用 lofty 既不达意也不搭配。\nJ) premature 义为\"过早的\"，与\"排除变量后仍成立\"语义反向\npremature 暗示结论下得太早、不可靠，而句中明显是肯定研究的稳健性，方向相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·It is __ that 形式主语句的"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) affect\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 could 后接动词原形\n【依据】affect the link = 影响这种关联\n上文说研究人员考虑了\"可能影响这种关联\"的多种因素，factors that could __(28)__ the\nlink, including whether participants had health conditions such as heart disease or\ncancer，举例就是疾病、抑郁等会干扰相关性的因素，正是 affect 的中性\"影响\"义。\n【竞争词】K) reconciled 义为\"调和、使一致\"，与\"干扰关联\"方向不符\nreconcile the link 几乎不成立；这里要表达的是健康状况会\"影响/干扰\"乐观与长寿之间的关\n联，而非\"调和\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 could 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) span\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 longer 后的名词中心语\n【依据】life span = 寿命，与 longer 搭配 = 更长寿命\n全文话题就是\"乐观与长寿\"，certain psychological factors may predict a longer life\n__(29)__.——a longer life span 即\"更长寿命\"，与 participants who reported the\nhighest levels of optimism were far more likely to live to age 85 or __(26)__. 形成主题\n呼应。\n【竞争词】N) spiral life spiral 不成搭配\nspiral 是\"螺旋\"，与 life 组合既不是英语常见搭配，也与\"更长寿命\"语义无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 longer 后的名词中心语"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) premature\n形容词\n【词性槽】of + 形容词 + 名词 death 的定语\n【依据】premature death = 过早死亡，医学/健康语境固定搭配\n上文 more optimistic people have a lower risk of developing chronic diseases 并列下\n来，a lower risk of __(30)__ death 的逻辑是\"较低的过早死亡风险\"，premature death 是\n医学常用术语，正好搭配。\n【竞争词】G) lofty 义\"崇高的\"，无法修饰 death\nlofty 与 death 在语义和感情色彩上都不兼容，没有 lofty death 这样的英语搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·of + 形容词 + 名词 death"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) specifically\n副词\n【词性槽】to + 副词 + 动词原形\n【依据】the first to specifically look at = 首个专门研究……\n前文已铺垫\"以往研究发现乐观降低慢性病/过早死亡风险\"，本句要点出新研究的独特贡献\n——the first to __(31)__ look at the relationship between optimism and longevity.，\n意为\"首项专门考察乐观与长寿关系的研究\"，specifically 正合此意。\n【竞争词】F) henceforth 时间副词\"今后\"，与\"首项研究\"语义反向\nhenceforth 表示\"自此以后\"，强调未来时间，与\"the first to\"描述既有研究身份不符。\nI) plausibly 义\"貌似可信地\"，弱化语气与首创性不符\nthe first to plausibly look 暗含\"看起来还行地看了一下\"，明显弱化原句强调研究首创性的\n语气。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— I plausibly 也是副词，且义\"貌似可信地\"听起来也学术。the\nfirst to __(31)__ look at the relationship 处，考生看到 plausibly 是副词、又有\"看起来合\n理\"的学术味，可能误选。防坑：本句强调研究的\"首创性 + 专门性\"，需要 specifically 这\n种聚焦副词；plausibly 反而削弱句意。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·to + 副词 + 动词原形"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) conceded\n动词\n【词性槽】主句缺谓语，主语 researchers 后\n【依据】concede that... = 承认（局限性）……\n下文 the link found in the new study was not as strong when they factored in the\neffects of certain health behaviors 是研究者对自家结论的限制性说明——承认在控制健\n康行为后关联减弱，正是 concede（承认局限/让步）的典型用法。\n【竞争词】K) reconciled 义为\"调和、使一致\"，不接 that 从句作宾语\nreconcile 常接 with/to 或接两件需调和的事物，不接 that 从句陈述发现；语义也与\"承认局\n限\"无关。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— K reconciled 与 C conceded 同为过去式动词形态，易\n混。The researchers __(32)__ that the link found in the new study was not as strong\n处，K reconciled 形态和位置都合（researchers reconciled that...），让考生误以为可\n填。防坑：reconcile 不接 that 从句，且无\"承认局限\"之义；这里需要带\"让步色彩\"的动\n词，concede 才是命题人想要的。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主句缺谓语，主语 researchers"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) foster\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 may 后接动词原形\n【依据】foster good habits 是常见搭配，意为\"培养好习惯\"\n句子推论是\"乐观可能促进/培养健康习惯，从而支撑健康\"，foster 在英语里与 habits、\ngrowth、development 等抽象名词搭配得最自然，optimism may __(33)__ good habits\nthat bolster health. 顺读为 optimism may foster good habits。\n【竞争词】A) affect 中性\"影响\"，但搭配方向不指向\"培养\"\naffect 多表中性或负面影响，与 good habits 搭配不自然，且无法准确表达\"乐观→催生好习\n惯\"的因果方向；另外 A 将在 28 题更需要它。\nK) reconciled 形态不对且语义不搭\nreconciled 是过去式/分词，紧接 may 后不合形态；语义\"使和解/调和\"也不与 good habits\n搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 may 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) correlation\n名词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a 后\n【依据】found a correlation 与下句\"未证明因果\"严丝合缝\nas 后给出原因 researchers did not prove for certain that optimism leads to a longer\nlife.——没有证明因果，只能说\"只发现了一种相关性\"，与统计语境里\"correlation ≠\ncausation\"的标准说法一致。\n【竞争词】L) span 义为\"跨度/期间\"，不与\"未证因果\"对应\na span 通常指 life span（寿命跨度），但这里要表达的是\"研究只发现 X 与 Y 之间的关系\"，不\n是寿命长度。\nN) spiral 义为\"螺旋\"，与本句逻辑无关\na spiral 多用于 a downward/upward spiral 等隐喻，与\"乐观与长寿的关系\"语境不沾边。\nO) trait 义为\"特征\"，35 题留给它更合身\na trait 指个人特征，与 found（研究发现的关系）不搭；而且 35 题 psychological __ 处需要\ntrait 表达\"心理特质\"，留给那道更贴。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定冠词 a 后"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affect", "B) beyond", "C) conceded", "D) correlation", "E) foster", "F) henceforth", "G) lofty", "H) noteworthy", "I) plausibly", "J) premature", "K) reconciled", "L) span", "M) specifically", "N) spiral", "O) trait"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) trait\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 psychological 后的中心词\n【依据】psychological trait = 心理特质，是固定搭配\n全文把乐观当作一种\"可促进健康、延长寿命\"的个人属性，optimism could serve as a\npsychological __(35)__ that promotes health and a longer life.——optimism 作为一种\npsychological trait 是心理学常用说法，词义与搭配双重契合。\n【竞争词】L) span psychological span 不成搭配\nspan 多搭配 life/time/attention（attention span），不与 psychological 构成自然搭配。\nN) spiral 义偏\"螺旋下降/上升\"，与\"促进健康的心理\"逻辑不符\npsychological spiral 多带负面意味（情绪螺旋），无法表达\"促进健康长寿\"的正向作用。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 psychological 后的"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Burberry's executives are trying hard to attribute their practice of destroying old products to miscalculated production.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高管把销毁归因于「误判产量」\n【定位】While there has been much outrage at the elitist connotation of burning goods rather than\nmaking them affordable, executives at the British fashion house are no doubt struggling to\ndefend how they miscalculated production. The waste has been put down to burning old\ncosmetic stock to make way for their new beauty range.\n尽管「宁可烧也不便宜卖」的精英主义意味招致了大量愤怒，英国这家时装公司的高管们无\n疑正费力地为自己「误判了产量」辩解。这次的浪费被归因于烧掉旧化妆品库存，以便给新\n美妆线腾出位置。\n【改写】executives are trying hard to attribute their practice of destroying old products ↔\nexecutives at the British fashion house are no doubt struggling to defend how they\nmiscalculated production\ntrying hard 与 struggling to 同义，attribute 与 defend how 同向\nmiscalculated production ↔ miscalculated production\n两侧用同一短语原样复现\n【辨邻】 A £28.6 million worth of stock was burnt last year 是事实陈述，没有任何\nexecutives 表态把它 attribute 到 miscalculated production 的内容，因此不能作为\n本题信息源。\nBurberry's annual report revealed that £28.6 million worth of stock was burnt last year. The news\nhas left investors and consumers outraged\nI 陈述句要求的是 attribute their practice of destroying old products to\nmiscalculated production，而本段是 Burberry denied that 与 has had an impact\non waste 的搭配，是反方向的「否认某种关联」，与「归因于误判产量」不构成等值\n关系。\nBurberry denied that switching to", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Selling products at a discount will do greater harm to luxury brands than destroying them.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】打折比销毁更伤奢侈品牌\n【定位】The practice of destroying unsold stock, and even rolls of unused fabric, is commonplace for\nluxury labels. Becoming too widely available at a cheaper price through discount stores\ndiscourages full-price sales.\n销毁未售出库存、甚至整卷未用过的面料，在奢侈品牌里很常见。如果商品通过折扣店以更\n低价大量铺货，反而会打击正价销售。\n【改写】Selling products at a discount ↔\nBecoming too widely available at a cheaper price through discount stores\ndiscount 与 at a cheaper price through discount stores 是同义改写\ndo greater harm to luxury brands than destroying them ↔ discourages full-price sales\n抽象伤害被具体化为「打击正价销售」\n【辨邻】 F Richemont, who owns several luxury brands, hit the headlines in May for taking\nback £437 million of watches for destruction 与 to avoid marked-down prices\n只指出「销毁是为了避免降价」，并没有把「打折 vs 销毁」的伤害程度做对比；陈述\n句需要的是 greater harm 的比较关系，本段不承担。\ntaking back £437 million of watches for destruction in the last two years to avoid marked-\ndown prices\nG burning goods rather than making them affordable 只是描述了批评者的视角「宁\n烧不便宜卖」，没有给出 do greater harm 的判断依据，因此不是本句来源。\nthere has been much outrage at the elitist connotation of burning goods rather than making\nthem affordable", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Imitated Burberry products discouraged luxury consumers from buying its genuine products.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】山寨 Burberry 产品劝退高端消费者\n【定位】Fifteen years ago, Burberry was at crisis point as their signature check pattern was widely\nimitated by cheap, imitation brands. It deterred luxury consumers who found their expensive\nclothing more closely associated with working-class youth culture than a prestigious heritage\nfashion house.\n十五年前，Burberry 正处危机：它的标志性格纹被廉价山寨品牌大量仿冒，劝退了那些高\n端消费者——这些消费者发现这种本应昂贵的衣服，更像和工薪青年文化挂钩，而非一家有\n底蕴的高端时装品牌。\n【改写】Imitated Burberry products ↔\ntheir signature check pattern was widely imitated by cheap, imitation brands\nimitated 同形复现，所指相同\ndiscouraged luxury consumers from buying its genuine products ↔\nIt deterred luxury consumers who found their expensive clothing more closely associated with\nworking-class youth culture\ndiscouraged 与 deterred 同义改写，配上「觉得不再高级」具体化\n【辨邻】 B 陈述句明确要求「被仿冒的产品」作为施害者，而本段说的是 Becoming too widely\navailable at a cheaper price through discount stores，是品牌自家产品过度铺货，\n不是山寨品对正品的冲击。\nBecoming too widely available at a cheaper price through discount stores discourages full-\nprice sales", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Staff members of a luxury brand may buy its old stock at cheaper prices, but they are not allowed to resell them.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】员工可低价购入老库存但不许转卖\n【定位】Ahmed Bouchfaa, who claimed to work for Louis Vuitton, responded that the brand holds\nsales of old stock for staff members twice a year. Items which have still not sold after several\nsales are destroyed. He goes on to disclose the strict guidelines around the employee sales:\nYou may buy gifts for someone, but they track each item, and if your gift ends up online they\nknow who to ask.\n声称在 Louis Vuitton 任职的 Ahmed Bouchfaa 回应称，该品牌每年办两次面向员工的旧\n库存特卖；若几次特卖之后仍卖不掉，就会被销毁。他进一步透露员工特卖的严格规定：你\n可以买来送人，但他们会追踪每一件商品，如果你送的礼物出现在网上，他们清楚该找谁\n问话。\n【改写】Staff members of a luxury brand may buy its old stock at cheaper prices ↔\nthe brand holds sales of old stock for staff members twice a year\n员工特卖即「员工低价购入旧库存」\nthey are not allowed to resell them ↔\nthey track each item, and if your gift ends up online they know who to ask\n追踪转售即禁止转售\n【辨邻】 D 本段员工是销毁链条的执行者，与陈述句中「低价购入并被禁止转卖」的内部特卖参与\n者完全是两类员工故事，不能混作同一情节。\nMy job was to toss items in boxes so they could be sent to be burned", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "In future traditional luxury brands will have to adapt their business strategies to the changing concepts of luxury.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】传统奢侈品牌需适应新的奢侈观\n【定位】Things like health, enlightenment, and social and environmental responsibility are the new\nluxuries. These all come from within, not without. That's the challenge that traditional luxury\nbrands will have to contend with in the mid- to long-term future.\n健康、觉悟、社会与环境责任，这些才是新的奢侈。它们都来自内在，而非外在。这正是传\n统奢侈品牌在中长期未来必须正视的挑战。\n【改写】traditional luxury brands will have to adapt their business strategies ↔\nThat's the challenge that traditional luxury brands will have to contend with in the mid- to\nlong-term future\nadapt strategies 与 contend with challenge 为同义改写\nthe changing concepts of luxury ↔\nThings like health, enlightenment, and social and environmental responsibility are the new\nluxuries\nchanging concepts 与 new luxuries 同义\nIn future ↔ in the mid- to long-term future\nin future 与 in the mid- to long-term future 同指「未来」\n【辨邻】 B 陈述句明确指向 In future traditional luxury brands will have to adapt（未来必须\n调整），而本段是 luxury brands rally around exclusivity to protect their business\ninterests 当下做法的描述，与「未来策略调整」是两个不同时间维度的陈述。\nluxury brands rally around exclusivity to protect their business interests, namely intellectual\nproperty and preservation of brand equity", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "One luxury brand employee quit her job because she simply couldn't bear to see the destruction of unsold products.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】员工因不忍销毁滞销品而辞职\n【定位】My job was to toss items in boxes so they could be sent to be burned. It was killing me inside\nbecause all that leather and fur went to waste and animals had died for nothing. I couldn't stay\nthere any longer, their business practices threw me off the roof. She has since worked with\nanother high-profile, luxury label.\n我的工作是把货品扔进箱子，然后送去焚烧。这让我心如刀割，因为那么多皮革皮草都被白\n白浪费，动物白死了。我再也待不下去了，他们的做法把我逼疯了。她此后转去了另一家知\n名奢侈品牌任职。\n【改写】One luxury brand employee quit her job ↔ I couldn't stay there any longer\ncouldn't stay 即 quit\nshe simply couldn't bear to see the destruction of unsold products ↔\nIt was killing me inside because all that leather and fur went to waste\nkilling me inside 对应 couldn't bear；waste 对应 destruction of unsold products\n【辨邻】 E Ahmed Bouchfaa, who claimed to work for Louis Vuitton 是在解释员工内部销售\n规则与追踪机制，不是「辞职出于受不了销毁」的故事，与陈述句要求不符。\nAhmed Bouchfaa, who claimed to work for Louis Vuitton, responded that the brand holds sales of\nold stock for staff members twice a year", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Destroying old stock is a practice not just of luxury brands but of less prestigious fashion brands.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】销毁库存不止奢侈品牌，普通品牌也做\n【定位】Richemont, who owns several luxury brands, hit the headlines in May for taking back £437\nmillion of watches for destruction in the last two years to avoid marked-down prices. It's not\njust luxury brands either. In October last year, a Danish TV show exposed H&M for burning\n12 tonnes of unsold clothing since 2013.\n拥有多个奢侈品牌的 Richemont 在五月登上头条，因其过去两年回收并销毁了价值 4.37 亿\n英镑的手表，以避免降价销售。这种做法不止奢侈品牌在做。去年十月，一档丹麦电视节目\n曝光了 H&M 自 2013 年起焚烧 12 吨未售衣物。\n【改写】Destroying old stock is a practice not just of luxury brands but of less prestigious fashion brands ↔\nIt's not just luxury brands either\nnot just luxury brands 同形对应，either 即 not just but 的另一种表达\nless prestigious fashion brands ↔ H&M for burning 12 tonnes of unsold clothing since 2013\n上位词的下位例化\n【辨邻】 C 陈述句要求文章提供 less prestigious fashion brands 也在做销毁的证据，但本段只\n给「为什么常见」的原因，没有列出大众品牌案例。\nAnother reason for the commonplace practice is a financial incentive for brands exporting goods\nto America", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Burberry is working with a partner to make full use of leather materials to reduce waste.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Burberry 与合作伙伴一起充分利用皮革减废\n【定位】Co-founder Kresse Wesling said, Late last year we launched an ambitious five-year\npartnership with the Burberry Foundation. The main aim of this is to scale our leather rescue\nproject, starting with off-cuts from the production of Burberry leather goods.\n联合创始人 Kresse Wesling 说：去年年底，我们和 Burberry 基金会启动了一项雄心勃勃\n的五年合作。它的主要目的是扩大我们的「皮革拯救项目」，从 Burberry 皮具生产的边角\n料做起。\n【改写】Burberry is working with a partner ↔\nLate last year we launched an ambitious five-year partnership with the Burberry Foundation\npartner 与 partnership 同根词；Burberry Foundation 代表 Burberry\nto make full use of leather materials to reduce waste ↔\nto scale our leather rescue project, starting with off-cuts from the production of Burberry\nleather goods\nrescue 加 off-cuts 即「对边角料做充分利用以减废」\n【辨邻】 I we have forged partnerships and committed support to innovative\norganizations to help reach this goal 只是宣称已经建立合作，并没有提及陈述句明\n确要求的 leather materials，因此不是本句直接来源。\nwe have forged partnerships and committed support to innovative organizations to help reach\nthis goal", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Burberry's plan to destroy its unsold products worth millions of dollars aroused public indignation.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】Burberry 销毁巨额未售品引公众愤慨\n【定位】Last week, Burberry's annual report revealed that £28.6 million worth of stock was burnt last\nyear. The news has left investors and consumers outraged but comes as little surprise to those\nin the fashion industry.\n上周，Burberry 的年报披露其去年焚烧了价值 2860 万英镑的库存。这则新闻让投资者和\n消费者愤怒不已，但对行业内人士而言并不令人意外。\n【改写】Burberry's plan to destroy its unsold products worth millions of dollars ↔\n£28.6 million worth of stock was burnt last year\nmillions of dollars 与 £28.6 million（具体金额）\naroused public indignation ↔ The news has left investors and consumers outraged\noutraged 等于 indignation；investors 加 consumers 共同构成 public\n【辨邻】 E One investor commenting on the Burberry figures was reportedly outraged\nthat the unsold goods were not even offered to investors before they were\ndestroyed 只是一位投资者的局部表态，而非陈述句要求的「公众愤慨」总评论，因\n此不是源头段。\nOne investor commenting on the Burberry figures was reportedly outraged that the unsold goods\nwere not even offered to investors before they were destroyed\nG 陈述句的 aroused public indignation 指向 plan to destroy unsold products\nworth millions of dollars 这条新闻本身；而 G 段的 outrage 指向「销毁而非打折」\n这种选择，属于该新闻引出的子话题，不是新闻总结句。\nthere has been much outrage at the elitist connotation of burning goods rather than making\nthem affordable", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Burberry's change of marketing strategy to make a product available as soon as consumers see it on the fashion show did not turn out to be as effective as expected.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「看秀即买」营销策略效果不如预期\n【定位】In September 2016, Burberry switched to a see now, buy now catwalk show format. The\nmove was a switch to leverage on the coverage of their fashion week show to make stock\navailable immediately to consumers. While Burberry announced record-breaking online reach\nand engagement, there has been little evidence to suggest that the strategy has had a\nsignificant effect on sales, particularly as the hype slows across the season.\n2016 年 9 月，Burberry 转向了「看秀即买」的秀场模式。此举意在借助时装周秀场的曝\n光，让消费者立刻就能买到商品。尽管 Burberry 宣称线上的触达与互动「破纪录」，但几\n乎没有证据表明该策略对销量有显著拉动，尤其当一整季中炒作热度逐渐降温后。\n【改写】Burberry's change of marketing strategy to make a product available as soon as consumers see it on\nthe fashion show\n↔ Burberry switched to a\n概念性描述对上专有营销名\ndid not turn out to be as effective as expected ↔\nthere has been little evidence to suggest that the strategy has had a significant effect on sales\nnot as effective as expected 与 little evidence of significant effect 同义\n【辨邻】 I 陈述句要求评价 effective as expected（是否如预期般有效），即销售效果；而\nBurberry denied that switching to 与 has had an impact on waste 谈的是是否影\n响浪费，不是销售效果，不可混作同段。\nBurberry denied that switching to", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author mainly discuss in the passage?", "options": ["A) The advantage of using social media in screening job candidates.", "B) The potentially invasive nature of social media in everyday life.", "C) Whether the benefits of social media outweigh the drawbacks.", "D) Whether social media should be used to screen job candidates."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the author mainly discuss in the passage? 直接问全文主要讨论什么，是主\n旨题最标准的提问形式。\n【拆句】简单陈述疑问句。主语 the author，谓语 discuss，状语 mainly 修饰 discuss，宾语用\nwhat 提前形成 wh-问句。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nThe advantage of using social media in screening job candidates 只对应 P5 的正\n面讨论，忽略了 P3P4 的风险与 P2 的议题本身——属于\"局部当全文\"。\nB) 偷换概念\nThe potentially invasive nature of social media in everyday life 把讨论范围扩大\n到\"日常生活中的侵入性\"，与全文聚焦的\"招聘场景\"不符——招聘场景是文本主线，而\n日常生活只是 P1 的背景铺垫。\nC) 偷换概念\nWhether the benefits of social media outweigh the drawbacks 像主旨，但全文权\n衡的不是\"社媒整体\"的利弊，而是\"用社媒筛人\"这一具体做法的利弊。把 screening 这\n个核心动作丢了。\nD) ✓ 正确\nshould employers make use of it when researching a potential candidate's background? ↔\nWhether social media should be used to screen job candidates\n选项 D 是 P2 议题句的标准名词化改写：should→Whether...should、主动→被动；主\n旨题的最高级别命中。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What might happen when social media is used to screen job candidates?", "options": ["A) Moral or legal issues might arise.", "B) Company reputation might suffer.", "C) Sensational information might surface.", "D) Hiring decisions might be complicated."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What might happen when social media is used to screen job candidates? 是对客观事\n实的提问，可在原文找到对应句作直接转述。\n【拆句】由 when 引导状语从句的特殊疑问句。主句 What might happen，从句 when social\nmedia is used to screen job candidates——被动式 is used，不定式 to screen 作目的状\n语。结构清楚，无理解陷阱。\n【定位】But there are consequences and potential legal risks involved too. When done inappropriately,\nsocial media screening can be considered unethical or even illegal.\n但这背后也存在后果与潜在的法律风险。操作不当时，社媒筛查甚至会被视为不道德、乃至\n违法。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncan be considered unethical or even illegal ↔ Moral or legal issues might arise\n预判的\"道德与法律问题\"在 A 选项以 Moral or legal issues 原词级对应，might arise\n对应 might happen 的情态可能性，正确项锁定为 A。\nB) 偷换概念\nCompany reputation might suffer 看似与 stain your company's reputation 沾边，\n但那是\"用得好可避免\"的正向情境，而 47 题问\"用社媒筛人可能发生什么\"的中性后果，\n不是公司声誉受损。\nC) 无中生有\nSensational information might surface 的\"耸动信息\"在 P3 中找不到任何对应；原文\n谈的是后果与风险，而非披露的内容\"耸人听闻\"。\nD) 偷换概念\nHiring decisions might be complicated 与 consequences and potential legal risks\n不对应——原文谈\"道德/法律风险\"，不是说决策变\"复杂\"；且 impact their hiring\ndecision in a negative way 说的是负面影响，不等于 complicated。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "When could online personal information be detrimental to candidates?", "options": ["A) When it is separated from context.", "B) When it is scrutinised by an employer.", "C) When it is magnified to a ruinous degree.", "D) When it is revealed to the human resources."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】When could online personal information be detrimental to candidates? 问的是在何种\n情形下产生不利后果，是对原文某条件句的直接提取。\n【拆句】When 引导的特殊疑问句。主语 online personal information，系动词 could be，表语\ndetrimental to candidates。表语形容词 detrimental 后接 to + 受损者，结构平铺。\n【定位】Pictures or comments on a private page that are taken out of context could ruin a perfectly\ngood candidate's chances of getting hired.\n私人主页上被断章取义的图片或评论，可能毁掉一位本来非常合格的候选人被录用的机会。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ntaken out of context ↔ separated from context\n预判强调\"脱离语境\"，A 项 separated from context 是 out of context 的标准替换，\n逐字对应。\nB) 答非所问\nscrutinised by an employer 来自 scrutinising a job candidate's private life——那是\n社媒筛查的定义，而非\"何时不利于候选人\"的触发条件。被审视本身并不必然有害，断\n章取义时才有害。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 ruin a perfectly good candidate's chances 只说\"会毁掉机会\"，没有\"信息被放\n大\"的过程；ruinous 这个形容词在 C 项被嫁接到\"放大程度\"上，与原文不符。\nD) 张冠李戴\nrevealed to the human resources 与 ask human resources for help 沾边——HR 是\n解决方案的一环，不是\"在线信息何时伤害候选人\"的条件。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "How can employers use social media information to their advantage while avoiding unnecessary risks?", "options": ["A) By tipping the delicate balance.", "B) By using it in a legitimate way.", "C) By keeping personal information on record.", "D) By separating relevant from irrelevant data."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】How can employers use social media information to their advantage while avoiding\nunnecessary risks? 直接问\"用什么办法\"，是对方法步骤的事实提问。\n【拆句】How 引导的特殊疑问句。主语 employers，谓语 use social media information，状语 to\ntheir advantage，伴随状语 while avoiding unnecessary risks——while + 现在分词构成\n方式状语，注意 while 在这里不是\"虽然\"而是\"同时\"。\n【定位】While there are things that you shouldn't see, there are some things that can be lawfully\nconsidered—making it a valuable source of relevant information too. Using social media\nscreening appropriately can help ensure that you don't hire a toxic employee who will cost\nyou money or stain your company's reputation. Consider the lawful side of this process and\nyou may be able to hire the best employee ever.\n虽然有些东西你不该看，但也有一些东西可以合法考量——这让它成为相关信息的宝贵来\n源。恰当地使用社媒筛查能帮你避免雇到那种花你钱、损公司声誉的\"有毒员工\"。考虑这一\n过程的合法面，你也许能聘到最优秀的员工。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\ntipping the delicate balance 来自 There is a delicate balance.——原文要的是\"保持\n平衡\"，tip 是\"打破/倾斜\"平衡，与原文意图相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\ncan be lawfully considered ↔ By using it in a legitimate way\nUsing social media screening appropriately ↔ using it in a legitimate way\n预判强调\"在合法/恰当边界内使用\"，B 项 in a legitimate way 直接对应——副词性短\n语 legitimate way 是 lawfully／appropriately 的同义改写。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文从未提到\"把个人信息存档\"作为方法；document decisions 讲的是\"记录决定\"，不\n是\"留存候选人个人信息\"。\nD) 无中生有\n原文虽出现 relevant information，但只用 relevant 修饰 information，未指示雇主\n要\"亲自分离 relevant 与 irrelevant 数据\"这一动作。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author suggest doing before screening job candidates on social media?", "options": ["A) Hiring professionals to navigate the whole process.", "B) Anticipating potential risks involved in the process.", "C) Seeking advice from management and legal experts.", "D) Stipulating a set of rules for asking specific questions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest doing before screening job candidates on social\nmedia? 直接问作者建议的动作内容，可在文末建议段找到对应句。\n【拆句】What 引导的特殊疑问句，含双层动作链：主语 the author，谓语 suggest，宾语 doing，\n时间状语 before screening job candidates on social media——doing 后接 before +\nV-ing 的时序结构。注意 suggest 后跟动名词，不跟不定式。\n【定位】But before using social media to screen job candidates, consulting with management and\nlegal teams beforehand is essential in order to comply with all laws.\n但在用社交媒体筛选求职者之前，事先咨询管理层与法务团队是必要的，这样才能合乎一切\n法律。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nHiring professionals to navigate the whole process 借用 professionally and\nresponsibly 与 navigating it，但原文是说\"由 HR 帮忙\"，且强调\"事先咨询\"，没有让雇\n主\"另请专业人士主导\"。\nB) 张冠李戴\nAnticipating potential risks involved in the process 借用 potential legal risks\ninvolved，但那是\"陈述风险存在\"的句子，不是\"作者建议你先做什么\"。50 题问的是\nsuggest doing 的具体动作。\nC) ✓ 正确\nconsulting with management and legal teams beforehand ↔\nSeeking advice from management and legal experts\n预判中的\"管理层 + 法务\"两个对象，与 management and legal experts 一一对应；动\n作词 Seeking advice 与 consulting with 同义。\nD) 偷换概念\nStipulating a set of rules for asking specific questions 借用 Companies should\nstipulate that they will never ask for passwords——原文 stipulate 的对象是\"绝不索\n要密码\"，不是\"为提问设定规则\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What trend has been observed in a report?", "options": ["A) Food manufacturers, rising awareness of product safety.", "B) Food manufacturers, changing strategies to bolster profits.", "C) Consumers, growing demand for eye-catching food labels.", "D) Consumers, increasing desire for clear product information."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What trend has been observed in a report 是问一份报告里观察到的具体趋势，属\n于可直接回原文找事实陈述的题。\n【拆句】题干 What trend has been observed in a report 是一个被动语态的特殊疑问句，主干是\n\"What trend has been observed\"，介词短语 \"in a report\" 限定观察发生在那份报告之\n内。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】One report found that 39 percent of consumers would switch from the brands they currently\nbuy to others that provide clearer, more accurate product information.\n一份报告发现，39% 的消费者会从他们目前购买的品牌转向那些能提供更清晰、更准确产\n品信息的品牌。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nFood manufacturers, rising awareness of product safety 把趋势主体偷换成了制造\n商，且原文 One report found that 39 percent of consumers 明确主体是消费者。\nB) 答非所问\nFood manufacturers, changing strategies to bolster profits 描述的是原文 they\\'re\ndoing so with an eye towards…bolstering profits 里制造商的反应，而非那份 report\n观察到的 trend。题干 What trend has been observed in a report 指向报告里的发\n现，不是报告之外的厂商动作。\nC) 偷换概念\nConsumers, growing demand for eye-catching food labels 把原文中消费者要的\n\"clearer, more accurate\" 偷换成了 \"eye-catching\"（吸睛的），并把对 \"information\"\n的诉求换成对 \"labels\" 的诉求——视觉冲击力不等于信息清晰。\nD) ✓ 正确\n39 percent of consumers would switch from the brands they currently buy to others that provide\nclearer, more accurate product information\n↔ increasing desire for clear product information\nconsumers are increasingly demanding more information about what\\'s in their food ↔\nConsumers, increasing desire for clear product information\n预判出的两个要素（主体是消费者、内容是清晰准确的产品信息）与 Consumers,\nincreasing desire for clear product information 逐一对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author say is manufacturers' new marketing strategy?", "options": ["A) Stressing the absence of certain elements in their products.", "B) Articulating the unique nutritional value of their products.", "C) Supplying detailed information of their products.", "D) Designing transparent labels for their products."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say is manufacturers\\' new marketing strategy 用 \"What\ndoes the author say\" 引导，问的是作者直接陈述的内容。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say is manufacturers\\' new marketing strategy 是一个嵌\n套疑问句：主问句 \"What does the author say\" 后接一个省略 that 的宾语从句，其内核为\n\"X is Y\"，求的是 X（即策略的内容）。无陷阱。\n【定位】But the marketing strategy in response to this consumer demand has gone beyond articulating\nwhat is in a product, to labeling what is NOT in the food.\n但回应这种消费需求的营销策略已经不再止于说明产品里有什么，而是去标注食品里没有\n什么。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nlabeling what is NOT in the food ↔\nStressing the absence of certain elements in their products\nThese labels are known as \"absence claims\" labels ↔\nStressing the absence of certain elements in their products\nStressing the absence of certain elements in their products 的核心 \"absence of\ncertain elements\" 恰好就是预判的 \"没有什么\"。\nB) 无中生有\nArticulating the unique nutritional value of their products 提到的 \"unique\nnutritional value\"（独特营养价值）在原文完全无对应，属于在 articulating 一词上做\n的无中生有延伸。\nC) 与原文相反\nSupplying detailed information of their products 描述的是 articulating what is in\na product 这种 \"旧\" 策略，正是 But 之前被作者用 \"has gone beyond\" 否定掉的部\n分，不是 \"new\" 策略。\nD) 偷换概念\nDesigning transparent labels for their products 的 \"transparent labels\"（透明标\n签）借用了原文 If consumers say they want transparency 中 \"transparency\"，但\ntransparency 描述的是消费者诉求，并非作者所说的 \"new marketing strategy\" 的\n内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What point does the author make about non-GMO labels?", "options": ["A) They are increasingly attracting customers' attention.", "B) They create lots of trouble for GMO food producers.", "C) They should be used more for vegetables and milk.", "D) They cause anxiety about food among consumers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What point does the author make about non-GMO labels 问的是作者就某一具体\n对象表达的观点，可在原文锁定句中找到原据，归为细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What point does the author make about non-GMO labels 中 \"make a point\" 是\n固定搭配，意为 \"提出/表达一个观点\"，介词 \"about\" 后接观点对象。结构简洁，无陷阱。\n【定位】Some dairy companies are using the \"non-GMO\" label on their milk, despite the fact that all\nmilk is naturally GMO-free, another label that creates unnecessary fear around food.\n一些乳制品公司在他们的牛奶上使用 \"非转基因\" 标签，尽管所有牛奶天然就不含转基因\n——这是又一个在食品周围制造不必要恐惧的标签。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey are increasingly attracting customers\\' attention 描述的 \"吸引注意\" 在 P3 没\n有任何对应；原文谈的是制造恐惧，不是吸引眼球。\nB) 无中生有\nThey create lots of trouble for GMO food producers 把目标受影响对象写成 GMO\n食品生产商，原文只谈消费者层面的恐惧，未提对 GMO 生产商的麻烦。\nC) 与原文相反\nThey should be used more for vegetables and milk 借用了 P3 例证中的 tomatoes\n与 milk，但作者意图恰恰是批评而非鼓励这种标签的使用——\"should be used more\"\n与作者立场相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nanother label that creates unnecessary fear around food ↔\nThey cause anxiety about food among consumers\n预判落点是 creates unnecessary fear，选项 cause anxiety about food 与之核心语义\n全等。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say absence claims labels will do to food manufacturers?", "options": ["A) Cause changes in their marketing strategies.", "B) Help remove stigma around their products.", "C) Erode consumer trust and reduce sales.", "D) Decrease support from food scientists."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say absence claims labels will do to food manufacturers\n问的是 \"会对食品制造商造成什么\"，是可逐字回原文核对的细节。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say absence claims labels will do to food manufacturers\n是嵌套疑问句：主问句 \"What does the author say\"，宾语从句中 \"absence claims\nlabels will do to food manufacturers\" 主谓宾完整，\"will do\" 谓语带将来时态，问的是未\n来后果。\n【定位】For food manufacturers, it will mean damaged consumer trust and lower sales for everyone.\n对食品制造商而言，这（指 absence claims labels）将意味着消费者信任受损和销量普遍\n下降。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nCause changes in their marketing strategies 写的是营销策略的变化，原文未提制造\n商会因此改变策略；且题干问 \"will do to manufacturers\" 是问对制造商造成的伤害，\n不是问制造商的反应。\nB) 与原文相反\nHelp remove stigma around their products 与原文 absence claims labels can\ncreate a stigma around foods 完全相反——原文是 create a stigma（制造污名\n化），选项却说 remove stigma（去除污名化）。\nC) ✓ 正确\ndamaged consumer trust and lower sales ↔ Erode consumer trust and reduce sales\neroding consumer trust in not just a single product, but the entire food business ↔\nErode consumer trust and reduce sales\n预判的 \"信任受损 + 销量下降\" 与 Erode consumer trust and reduce sales 一一对\n应，连并列连词 \"and\" 都保留。\nD) 无中生有\nDecrease support from food scientists 提及的 \"food scientists\" 在原文中从未出\n现，属于无中生有；原文 there is no scientific evidence 只是说没有科学证据证明这些\n食品有害，并非谈科学家的支持度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest food manufacturers do?", "options": ["A) Take measures to lower the cost of food products.", "B) Exercise caution about the use of absence claims.", "C) Welcome new innovations in food processing.", "D) Promote efficiency and increase food variety."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author suggest food manufacturers do 问的是作者对制造商的具\n体建议，可在原文锁定句找到对应，归为细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author suggest food manufacturers do 是嵌套疑问句：主问\n\"What does the author suggest\"，宾语从句 \"food manufacturers do\" 用了 suggest 后\n从句的虚拟语气（动词原形 do），中国考生易把 \"do\" 误以为漏字，需点明这是语法规范。\n【定位】It\\'s clear that food manufacturers must tread carefully when it comes to using\nabsence claims.\n很明显，在使用 \"absence claims\" 标签这件事上，食品制造商必须谨慎行事。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nTake measures to lower the cost of food products 涉及降低食品成本，原文 more\ncostly food products 只是把高成本列为如果继续使用 absence claims 将会出现的后\n果，不是作者给出的建议。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfood manufacturers must tread carefully when it comes to using absence claims ↔\nExercise caution about the use of absence claims\nExercise caution about the use of absence claims 与预判一一对应，且 \"absence\nclaims\" 在选项与原文中原词复现。\nC) 过度推断\nWelcome new innovations in food processing 把 \"欢迎创新\" 当作建议；但原文 this\nverbal trick sends a message that innovations in farming and food processing\nare unwelcome 是说 absence claims 这种文字游戏暗示了 \"不欢迎创新\"，作者并未直\n接建议厂商去 welcome innovations。\nD) 过度推断\nPromote efficiency and increase food variety 把 \"提高效率与品类多样\" 当建议，原\n文 less efficiency, fewer choices for consumers 只是把 \"效率降低、选择减少\" 列为继\n续使用 absence claims 的负面后果，并未直接建议厂商去主动提升效率与品类。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
